const CS3345_STUDY_GUIDE_DATA = {"version":1,"sha256":"2d9c5b2af951cfee3e5ba16c9a06ce92739c97f54a699f8f1ccd334f2f9639b4","frontMatter":"title: \"CS 3345 — Study Guide\"\nsource_pdf: \"3345QuizStudyGuide(1).pdf\"\nsource_guide_pages: 59\ntopic_count: 20\nformat_version: 1\nfigure_format: \"embedded PNG data URIs (offline, self-contained)\"","intro":"\n# CS 3345 — Study Guide\n\u003ca id=\"study-guide-home\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\nAssigned-reading summaries and worked examples\n\nAll 20 syllabus topics - heaps and hashing, sorting, graph procedures, project timing, network flow, and minimum spanning trees.\n\nPrepared November 2, 2026. Textbook-style explanations use circular tree nodes, connected edges, pass tables, and algorithm-state tables. Every textbook example has a verified figure or page reference; original practice examples are labeled.\n\n### How to Use This Guide\nRead a topic's summary, work its example without looking at the final state, then complete independent website practice. Follow with 3-5 focused written questions. Correct gaps immediately, then move on; return later for independent retention rather than counting a copied retry as mastery.\n\nCurrent priority: Heapsort -\u003e Prim's Algorithm -\u003e PERT -\u003e Shortest-Path Algorithms -\u003e Network Flow. The contents retain the full syllabus-topic order so you can quickly find any section.\n\nParticular exam relevance: BuildHeap and its linear-time explanation; explaining heap insertion code; changing heapsort direction; tracing Prim; computing PERT latest times; choosing flow versus shortest paths. Past quiz structure guides emphasis, not certainty.\n\n*Source base: the supplied Weiss textbook, l13a.pdf, newfiboheap.pdf, Syllabus Version 2, and Old Quiz 2. Source limitations and attribution are listed at the end.*\n## Contents\nAll page numbers in this table refer to the original 59-page study guide, not the Weiss textbook.\n\n| Topic | Original guide page |\n| --- | ---: |\n| [01 Binary Heaps](#binary-heaps) | 3 |\n| [02 Hashing, including Cuckoo Hashing](#hashing) | 6 |\n| [03 d-Heaps](#d-heaps) | 8 |\n| [04 Leftist Heaps](#leftist-heaps) | 10 |\n| [05 Binomial Trees \u0026 Binomial Queues](#binomial-trees-binomial-queues) | 15 |\n| [06 Fibonacci \u0026 Pairing Heaps](#fibonacci-pairing-heaps) | 18 |\n| [07 Insertion Sort](#insertion-sort) | 20 |\n| [08 Merge Sort](#merge-sort) | 22 |\n| [09 Heapsort](#heapsort) | 25 |\n| [10 Quicksort](#quicksort) | 28 |\n| [11 Sorting Lower Bounds](#sorting-lower-bounds) | 30 |\n| [12 External Sorting](#external-sorting) | 32 |\n| [13 Graph Fundamentals](#graph-fundamentals) | 34 |\n| [14 Topological Sort / DAGs](#topological-sort-dags) | 36 |\n| [15 Dijkstra's Algorithm](#dijkstras-algorithm) | 38 |\n| [16 Shortest-Path Algorithms](#shortest-path-algorithms) | 41 |\n| [17 Activity Graphs](#activity-graphs) | 44 |\n| [18 PERT / Critical-Path Timing](#pert-critical-path) | 47 |\n| [19 Network Flow](#network-flow) | 50 |\n| [20 Prim's Algorithm](#prims-algorithm) | 54 |\n| [Quick Reference](#quick-reference) | 57 |\n| [Sources and Attribution](#sources) | 59 |\n\n**Indexing reminder:** The textbook binary priority queue uses positions starting at 1; its heapsort array starts at 0. The guide states the convention where it affects a formula or worked trace.\n\n**Notation:** N = items, V = vertices, E = edges; inf = infinity. Tree height counts edges, so a leaf has height zero. Leftist NPL(null) = −1. Runtime logarithms are base two unless a branching-factor base is shown.\n\n\u003c!-- Import guidance: Each topic begins with a stable topic-id comment and explicit anchor. Subsections have source-guide-page and section-role comments. Figures and equations are embedded directly as PNG data URIs, so no external image files are required. Preserve every table, attribution, and example when embedding in the standalone website. --\u003e\n","sections":[{"id":"binary-heaps","number":1,"title":"Binary Heaps","page":3,"markdown":"## 01 — Binary Heaps\n\u003ca id=\"binary-heaps\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: binary-heaps; topic-number: 01; source-guide-start-page: 3 --\u003e\n### Structure, operations, and the Java insertion explanation\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 3 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 225-232, 234-237; Figs. 6.6-6.8, 6.14.*\n\nA **priority queue** supports insertion and access/removal of a minimum-priority item. It orders removal by priority rather than arrival time. An unsorted list makes insertion O(1) but Delete Min O(N); keeping the list sorted reverses that basic tradeoff.\n\n#### Two independent heap properties\n\n**Structure:** a complete binary tree fills each level except possibly the last, which fills from left to right. **Min-heap order:** every parent is \u0026lt;= each existing child. Siblings need not be sorted. A valid heap array is generally not a sorted array.\n\nThe priority-queue implementation is **one-based**: parent(i)=floor(i/2), children 2i and 2i+1, last non-leaf floor(N/2). Leaves occupy floor(N/2)+1 through N. Height is floor(log2 N), counting edges.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"3\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"One-based binary heap: parent floor(i/2), children 2i and 2i+1, last internal floor(N/2)\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n#### Insert: preserve shape, then repair upward\n\nAppend at position N+1. Save x and move larger parents down into the hole until x reaches the root or a parent \u0026lt;= x. Then place x in the hole. Worst percolation is O(log N); average percolation is O(1) under the reading's assumptions, with capacity available. Resizing can make an individual insertion more expensive.\n\n**Insert percolation: O(log N) worst case, O(1) average under the stated assumptions**\n\nIn Fig. 6.8, ++currentSize creates the next hole. array[0]=x is a sentinel: at the root, integer parent index 1/2 is 0, so comparing x with itself stops the strict-less loop. Copying each parent down and writing x once uses d+1 element assignments across d levels, versus about 3d for swaps (excluding sentinel setup).\n\n#### Delete Min and key changes\n\nSave/remove the root minimum; use the final active key to fill its vacancy and remove that key's old final position. This preserves completeness. Move the smaller existing child upward until the replacement fits above every child or reaches a leaf. Delete Min is O(log N) worst case and on average in the reading.\n\n**Delete Min: O(log N)**\n\nAt a known position, **decreaseKey** can require percolate-up; **increaseKey** can require percolate-down. Delete a selected item by making it smaller than every other key, moving it to the root, then calling Delete Min. Without its position or another index, finding an arbitrary key can take O(N).\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY DISTINCTION**\n\n\u003e Copying items into consecutive array positions establishes complete-tree shape. Calling buildHeap then establishes heap order; it does not fully sort the array or change its indexing convention.\n\n### Worked textbook example: bottom-up BuildHeap\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 4 --\u003e\n*Redrawn from Weiss, Figs. 6.15-6.18, pp. 236-237. Arrays are level-order traversals.*\n\n**Problem.** Construct a min-heap from the textbook's 15 initial keys using bottom-up BuildHeap. Start at floor(15/2)=7 and work backward to 1. Complete each downward repair before changing the starting node.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"4\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Initial complete binary tree before bottom-up BuildHeap\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Initial complete tree: the shape is correct, but several parent-child pairs violate min-heap order.*\n\n| Starting node (1-based) | Array after its complete percolate-down |\n| --- | --- |\n| 7 | 150, 80, 40, 30, 10, 70, 110, 100, 20, 90, 60, 50, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 6 | 150, 80, 40, 30, 10, 50, 110, 100, 20, 90, 60, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 5 | 150, 80, 40, 30, 10, 50, 110, 100, 20, 90, 60, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 4 | 150, 80, 40, 20, 10, 50, 110, 100, 30, 90, 60, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 3 | 150, 80, 40, 20, 10, 50, 110, 100, 30, 90, 60, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 2 | 150, 10, 40, 20, 60, 50, 110, 100, 30, 90, 80, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n| 1 | 10, 20, 40, 30, 60, 50, 110, 100, 150, 90, 80, 70, 120, 140, 130 |\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"4\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Final min-heap after bottom-up BuildHeap\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Final min-heap (Fig. 6.18, right, p. 237). Parents are centered above their child positions; keys are not globally sorted.*\n\n### Why BuildHeap is linear; insertion and deletion traces\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 5 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Theorem 6.1, pp. 235-237; Figs. 6.6-6.11, pp. 230-232.*\n\n#### The invariant and the cost argument\n\nLeaves are already heaps. Working backward ensures both **entire child subtrees** satisfy heap order before their parent percolates down. Processing the root first lacks that guarantee: later subtree changes can move a smaller key beneath a parent already processed.\n\nA single percolate-down can cost O(log N), but most nodes cannot travel that far. About N/2 nodes are leaves, about N/4 have height one, N/8 have height two, and so on. Summing height-dependent work is O(N). Repeated insertion instead has O(N log N) total worst-case time.\n\n**Bottom-up BuildHeap: O(N) | N repeated insertions: O(N log N) worst case**\n\n| Node height | Number of nodes in a perfect height-3 tree | Height contribution |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 3 | 1 | 1*3 = 3 |\n| 2 | 2 | 2*2 = 4 |\n| 1 | 4 | 4*1 = 4 |\n| 0 (leaves) | 8 | 8*0 = 0 |\n| Total | 15 | 11 |\n\nFor a perfect tree, the sum of node heights is N-(h+1). Here it is 15-4=11. This is a bound on downward **levels**, not the exact count of all machine instructions. Big-O describes growth over different input sizes; it is not chosen by comparing a small numerical total with log N.\n\n#### Insert 14: textbook percolate-up example\n\nBefore insertion: [13,21,16,24,31,19,68,65,26,32]. Create the hole at position 11. Move 31 from 5 to 11, then 21 from 2 to 5. Since parent 13 \u0026lt;= 14, place 14 at 2. Result: [13,14,16,24,21,19,68,65,26,32,31].\n\n*Textbook data and insertion sequence: Figs. 6.6-6.7, p. 230.*\n\n#### Delete 13 from that result\n\nSave final key 31 and shrink to ten active positions. Move 14 to the root, 21 into position 2, then 31 fits in position 5 above its existing child 32. Result: [14,21,16,24,31,19,68,65,26,32].\n\n*Same textbook Delete Min example: Figs. 6.9-6.11, pp. 231-232. This guide explains the procedure without importing the unassigned p. 233 code listing.*\n\n\u003e **REVIEW YOUR RECENT MCQ GAPS**\n\n\u003e Distinguish heap order from complete-tree shape and full sorting. For one insertion, remember worst O(log N) versus average O(1) under the stated assumptions. In a height sum, every leaf contributes zero.\n\n"},{"id":"hashing","number":2,"title":"Hashing, including Cuckoo Hashing","page":6,"markdown":"## 02 — Hashing, including Cuckoo Hashing\n\u003ca id=\"hashing\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: hashing; topic-number: 02; source-guide-start-page: 6 --\u003e\n### Candidate locations, displacement, and guarantees\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 6 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 195, 205; Charles Chen, An Overview of Cuckoo Hashing (l13a.pdf), pp. 1-12.*\n\nA dictionary stores key-value records. Hashing maps a key to candidate storage positions; **collisions** occur when distinct keys share a position. Load factor for a table is n/r (items/buckets). For two cuckoo tables, distinguish each table's size r from total capacity 2r.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"6\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Load factor equals items divided by buckets\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n#### Collision policies in the supplied handout\n\n**Chaining:** colliding keys share a bucket list; a long list gives linear worst-case lookup. **Linear probing:** inspect consecutive cells, wrapping at the end. A true empty cell ends an unsuccessful search, while a deletion marker must permit searching onward. **Double hashing:** a second hash supplies the probe step. **Two-way chaining:** choose the shorter of two candidate bucket lists; lookups may inspect both lists.\n\n#### Cuckoo invariant and insertion\n\nIn the two-table scheme, key x occupies exactly one of **T1[h1(x)] or T2[h2(x)]**. Lookup checks at most two cells, giving O(1) worst-case time. Deletion clears the matching cell; no probe-chain tombstone is required.\n\n**Legal locations: T1[h1(x)] or T2[h2(x)] | lookup/delete: O(1) worst case**\n\nInsertion first checks duplicates. Place x in a legal cell; evict its occupant, then move that occupant to its other legal cell. Continue until an empty legal cell is reached. A displacement limit prevents endless cycling. If the limit is exceeded, select new hash functions and rebuild with **all keys, including the currently displaced key**.\n\nUnder the handout's hash-family and spare-capacity assumptions, insertion is expected amortized O(1), including rebuilding. This is not a constant worst-case bound for each insertion. Geometric resizing has O(n) aggregate copying work because n+n/2+n/4+... is O(n). The handout uses r \u003e= (1+epsilon)n per table for its analysis.\n\n**Cuckoo insert: expected amortized O(1), under the stated hash/load assumptions**\n\n#### The other material on the assigned textbook pages\n\nThe end of the **perfect hashing** discussion assumes a known static key set and builds two-level tables for two-probe lookup. Page 205's public cuckoo insert rejects duplicates, expands at its configured load threshold, then calls its insertion helper. **Hopscotch hashing** bounds each key's displacement within a fixed neighborhood of its home location, supporting bounded lookup and locality.\n\nA (c,k)-universal hash family bounds joint outcomes for k distinct keys. The cuckoo analysis needs more than pairwise collision information. The handout's proof classifies displacement patterns and bounds long chains; its experiments emphasize implementation, cache locality, load, and choice of hash functions.\n\n\u003e **GUARANTEE WORDING**\n\n\u003e State both the operation and the type of bound: cuckoo lookup/deletion are worst-case O(1); insertion is expected amortized O(1) under the stated assumptions. Do not claim collisions are impossible.\n\n### Worked textbook placement and an insertion chain\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 7 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 5.25, p. 195; public insert in Fig. 5.42, p. 205. Displacement method: l13a.pdf, p. 4.*\n\n**Textbook example.** Six keys have the legal cells listed below. The small tables illustrate placement rather than the spare-capacity assumptions of the analysis.\n\n| Key | Table 1 candidate | Table 2 candidate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 0 | 2 |\n| B | 0 | 0 |\n| C | 1 | 4 |\n| D | 1 | 0 |\n| E | 3 | 2 |\n| F | 3 | 4 |\n| Index | Table 1 | Table 2 |\n| 0 | B | D |\n| 1 | C | empty |\n| 2 | empty | A |\n| 3 | E | empty |\n| 4 | empty | F |\n\n*Reformatted from Fig. 5.25, p. 195. Each shown key occupies one of its two legal cells.*\n\n#### Original worked insertion sequence using those textbook candidates\n\nBegin with empty tables and insert A,B,C,D,E,F, always trying Table 1 first. A enters T1[0]. B evicts A from T1[0], and A moves to T2[2]. C enters T1[1]. D evicts C, which moves to T2[4]. E enters T1[3]. F evicts E, which evicts A from T2[2]. A evicts B from T1[0], and B enters empty T2[0].\n\nThis valid final arrangement is T1=[A,D,empty,F,empty] and T2=[B,empty,E,empty,C]. It differs from the displayed textbook placement, which is also valid. The invariant determines legal cells, not a unique arrangement.\n\n#### Why rebuilding may be necessary\n\nIf three distinct keys have exactly the same two candidate cells, only two can fit. No amount of displacement fixes that shortage under the same hash functions. Rehashing changes the candidates; it does not alter or discard the keys.\n\nFor a single table with 8 keys and 20 buckets, load factor is 8/20=0.4. Be explicit about the denominator when a problem instead asks occupancy across both cuckoo tables.\n\n"},{"id":"d-heaps","number":3,"title":"d-Heaps","page":8,"markdown":"## 03 — d-Heaps\n\u003ca id=\"d-heaps\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: d-heaps; topic-number: 03; source-guide-start-page: 8 --\u003e\n### Generalizing binary branching\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 8 --\u003e\n*Weiss, d-heap subsection on pp. 240-241, Fig. 6.19.*\n\nA d-ary heap preserves the complete-tree idea and parent-child heap order, but each internal node has up to d children. The final internal node can have fewer. More branching gives fewer levels, with height O(log_d N). Children are not required to be sorted relative to one another.\n\n#### One-based array formulas\n\nFor a nonroot position i, **parent(i)=floor((i-2)/d)+1**. The potential child block of parent i is **d(i-1)+2 through d(i-1)+d+1**. Include only indices \u0026lt;= N. The root has no parent; do not apply the nonroot formula as though it did.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"8\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"One-based d-heap: parent floor((i-2)/d)+1 and child block d(i-1)+2 through d(i-1)+d+1\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n#### Operations\n\n**Insert:** append at the next complete-tree position and compare with the single parent while moving upward. The worst percolation time is O(log_d N), with capacity available. Increasing d shortens that ancestor path without adding a scan of children.\n\n**Insert percolation: O(log_d N) worst case**\n\n**Delete Min:** replace the root using the final active key, shrink, then descend. At each level, find the smallest of all existing children and compare it with the replacement. Finding a minimum among d children requires d-1 comparisons, so total worst time is O(d log_d N).\n\n**Delete Min: O(d log_d N) worst case**\n\n**BuildHeap:** process non-leaves backward so each child subtree is already a heap. For fixed d the total construction time is O(N), using the same height-sum idea as binary BuildHeap.\n\n**Bottom-up BuildHeap: O(N) for fixed d**\n\n#### The tradeoff and motivation\n\nA larger d means fewer levels but more comparisons at each downward level. It especially favors workloads with many upward operations relative to deletions. Both insertion and deletion are O(log N) when d is a fixed constant; retaining d in the expression explains their differing costs.\n\nThe textbook also discusses arithmetic and memory-access effects: computed parent/child indices use multiplication or division by d, and shallower trees can reduce external-memory accesses. These practical effects mean an asymptotic expression alone does not identify the fastest implementation.\n\nOrdinary array heaps make efficient merging awkward because combining their storage/layout can require linear work. The next heap structures relax shape and use links to support faster merging.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Increasing d reduces the height. Insertion still compares with only one parent per level, while Delete Min must find the smallest among up to d children per level.\n\n### Worked 3-heap: indices, insertion, and deletion\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 9 --\u003e\n*Original practice example following Weiss, Fig. 6.19 and pp. 240-241; not a copied textbook figure.*\n\nUse **one-based indices** and the valid min-heap [4,9,7,12,15,18,20,11,13,16]. Its root has children at 2,3,4; node 2 has children 5,6,7; node 3 has existing children 8,9,10.\n\n| Index calculation | Substitution | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Parent of node 10 | floor((10-2)/3)+1 | 3 |\n| Potential children of node 3 | 3(3-1)+2 through 3(3-1)+4 | 8 through 10 |\n| Last non-leaf for N=10 | parent(10) | 3 |\n\n#### Insert key 3\n\nAppend 3 at position 11. Its parent is floor(9/3)+1=4, holding 12. Move 12 down to position 11. Position 4's parent is 1, holding 4, so move 4 down. Place 3 at the root.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"9\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"3-heap array after insertion\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*After insertion: positions 2,3,4 hold 9,7,4, all \u003e= the new root 3.*\n\n#### Delete Min from that resulting heap\n\nRemove 3, save final key 12, and shrink back to ten entries. Compare root children 9,7,4: move 4 to the root. Position 4 has no children because its first child would be index 11. Place 12 there.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"9\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"3-heap array after deletion\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n#### BuildHeap starting rule\n\nOn an arbitrary ten-key 3-heap array, start at node 3, then process 2 and 1. Node 3's child block is 8..10; node 2's is 5..7. Each repair compares all existing children, rather than applying the binary heap's two-child rule.\n\n\u003e **WATCH THE CONVENTION**\n\n\u003e Do not use floor(i/2) for a 3-heap. Also do not reuse these one-based formulas for a zero-based sorting array.\n\n"},{"id":"leftist-heaps","number":4,"title":"Leftist Heaps","page":10,"markdown":"## 04 — Leftist Heaps\n\u003ca id=\"leftist-heaps\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: leftist-heaps; topic-number: 04; source-guide-start-page: 10 --\u003e\n### NPL, merging, and creating a heap by insertion\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 10 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 241-245, Figs. 6.20-6.24; newfiboheap.pdf, pp. 2-3.*\n\nA leftist heap is a linked binary tree satisfying min-heap order and a different structural rule from a complete binary heap. It can have long left chains. **It does not have to be complete or balanced by total height.**\n\n#### What null path length measures\n\nNPL(v) is the shortest distance in edges from v to a descendant with fewer than two children. A node with zero or one child already qualifies, so its NPL is 0. Define NPL(null)=-1. Then **NPL(v)=1+min(NPL(left),NPL(right))** works uniformly.\n\n**NPL(null) = -1 | NPL(v) = 1 + min(NPL(left), NPL(right))**\n\nThe leftist property is **NPL(left)\u003e=NPL(right)** at every node. It compares path lengths, not child keys or subtree sizes. For example, child NPLs 2 and 0 give parent NPL 1, not 3.\n\n**Leftist rule: NPL(left) \u003e= NPL(right)**\n\n#### Merge is the fundamental operation\n\n1. If either heap is empty, return the other. 2. Keep the smaller root. 3. Recursively merge its **right subtree** with the other heap, and attach that result on the right. 4. If NPL(left)\u0026lt;NPL(right), swap the entire child subtrees. 5. Recompute NPL as 1+NPL(right), once leftist order is restored.\n\nMoving entire child subtrees preserves their internal heap order. A swap also preserves order at the parent because both child roots are already no smaller than it. Update NPL while unwinding the recursion, not just once at the overall root.\n\n#### Insert, Delete Min, and runtime\n\n**Insert:** create a singleton and merge it with the heap. **Delete Min:** remove the root and merge its two child heaps. To build from a sequence, start empty and finish each singleton merge before inserting the next key.\n\nA leftist tree with r **nodes** on its rightmost path contains at least 2^r-1 total nodes. Thus r\u0026lt;=floor(log2(N+1)). Merge follows short right paths with constant work per visited node, giving O(log N) worst-case time; insertion and Delete Min inherit that bound. Find Min is O(1).\n\n**Right path: r \u0026lt;= log2(N+1) | merge, insert, Delete Min: O(log N) worst case**\n\nThe supplemental notes use the name mind for the same shortest-path measure and discuss known-node decrease-key by cutting, repairing affected NPL values, and merging back. Direct handles avoid an unrelated linear search. The guide follows the correct smaller-root merge rule where prose/pseudocode typos appear in the notes.\n\n\u003e **YOUR WRITTEN-ANSWER TARGET**\n\n\u003e The leftist property keeps the right path short; merge works along that path. NPL is a shortest path to a node lacking two children, not the overall height or the number of null links.\n\n### Textbook merge: the two inputs and recursive choices\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 11 --\u003e\n*Redrawn from Weiss, Fig. 6.21, p. 243; recursive attachment in Figs. 6.22-6.23, pp. 243, 245.*\n\n**Problem.** Merge the two textbook leftist heaps. H1 is on the left, with root 3; H2 is on the right, with root 6. Small NPL labels are values for null path length, computed using NPL(null)=-1.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"11\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Two input leftist heaps with NPL annotations\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Textbook keys and edges, Fig. 6.21, p. 243. NPL labels are added for study.*\n\n| Comparison / return | Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| Roots 3 and 6 | Keep 3; recursively merge its right subtree rooted at 8 with H2. |\n| Roots 8 and 6 | Keep 6; recursively merge its right subtree rooted at 7 with the heap rooted at 8. |\n| Roots 7 and 8 | Keep 7; merge its right child 18 with the heap rooted at 8. |\n| Roots 18 and 8 | Keep 8; attach 18 to its empty right side. Its left child 17 has NPL 0, so no swap is needed. |\n| Return to 7 | Its right child 8 has NPL 1, while left child 37 has NPL 0: swap them. |\n| Return to 6 | Its child NPLs are both 1: keep their order and update NPL(6)=2. |\n| Return to 3 | Its left child 10 has NPL 1 and new right child 6 has NPL 2: swap them. |\n\nA lower key on the right is not itself a reason to swap children: both need only be \u003e= their parent. The swap decision is determined by **child NPLs**.\n\n### Final merged heap and a creation-from-empty example\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 12 --\u003e\n*Final textbook structure: Fig. 6.24, p. 245. Insertion sequence below is original practice.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"12\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Final merged leftist heap\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Result of merging the textbook H1 and H2. The final root swap places the larger-NPL subtree on the left.*\n\n#### Check the completed merge\n\nRoot 3 remains the minimum. Its left child 6 has NPL 2, while right child 10 has NPL 1, so the final root satisfies the leftist inequality. The left subtree of 6 is rooted at 12 and its right subtree at 7; both have NPL 1.\n\nThe entire merged heap has 16 nodes and NPL 2 at its root. Following right children gives 3,10,14, a three-node right path. The longer branch toward node 26 is allowed because leftist heaps do not require global height balance.\n\n\u003e **MERGE DOES NOT SORT SIBLINGS**\n\n\u003e After restoration, root 3 has left child 6 and right child 10, but that numerical left-to-right relationship is incidental. The required comparisons are parent \u0026lt;= child and NPL(left) \u003e= NPL(right).\n\n### Creating a heap from an insertion sequence\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 13 --\u003e\n*Original practice example following the singleton-merge method on Weiss, pp. 242-244, and newfiboheap.pdf, p. 3.*\n\n#### Create a leftist heap by inserting 10,3,8,21,14,17,6\n\nStart empty. Treat every incoming key as a singleton heap and merge it with the current heap. For example, after inserting 10 then 3, root 3 has left child 10; inserting 8 places it on the right. Every later insertion again follows the right-path merge and NPL repair rules.\n\n| Inserted key | Root | NPL(root) | Root left / right child |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 10 | 10 | 0 | null / null |\n| 3 | 3 | 0 | 10 / null |\n| 8 | 3 | 1 | 10 / 8 |\n| 21 | 3 | 1 | 10 / 8 |\n| 14 | 3 | 1 | 8 / 10 |\n| 17 | 3 | 1 | 8 / 10 |\n| 6 | 3 | 1 | 8 / 6 |\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"13\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Leftist heap after successive singleton insertions\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Original insertion example. A creation problem is solved by repeated singleton merges, not by bottom-up array BuildHeap.*\n\n### Paper solution for Leftist Delete Min\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 14 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** Root 2 has left child 4 (children 7 and 8) and right child 5 (left child 9 only). Delete the minimum.\n\n**Solution.** Remove 2. Merge roots 4 and 5, keeping 4. Merge its right subtree rooted at 8 with the subtree rooted at 5. Keep 5 and attach 8 as its right child. Its child NPLs are both 0, so NPL(5)=1. Back at 4, the left child 7 has NPL 0 and the new right child 5 has NPL 1. Swap those whole subtrees. NPL(4)=1+0=1.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"14\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Leftist heap after Delete Min\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n**Final answer.** Removed key 2. Root 4 has left child 5 with children 9 and 8, and right child 7.\n\n"},{"id":"binomial-trees-binomial-queues","number":5,"title":"Binomial Trees \u0026 Binomial Queues","page":15,"markdown":"## 05 — Binomial Trees \u0026 Binomial Queues\n\u003ca id=\"binomial-trees-binomial-queues\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: binomial-trees-binomial-queues; topic-number: 05; source-guide-start-page: 15 --\u003e\n### Tree orders, binary carries, and queue operations\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 15 --\u003e\n*Weiss, p. 252, pp. 253-259, p. 261; newfiboheap.pdf, pp. 3-6.*\n\nA binomial queue is a **forest** of heap-ordered binomial trees, with at most one tree of each order. B0 is a singleton. Link two Bk trees to make B(k+1), retaining the smaller root as parent and attaching the larger-root tree as a child.\n\nBk has **2^k nodes**, height k, and root degree k. Its root children are B(k-1),...,B0, one of each; the number of nodes at depth d is the binomial coefficient choose(k,d). This exact recursive shape is more restrictive than ordinary heap order.\n\n**B_k: 2^k nodes | height k | root degree k | depth d has choose(k,d) nodes**\n\n#### Why binary representation describes the forest\n\nOrder k contributes 2^k nodes. A queue of size 13 is 1101 in binary and contains B3,B2,B0: 8+4+1. Unique orders give logarithmically many root slots. Two same-order trees link like a binary carry; three same-order trees leave one in the current slot and carry a link of the other two.\n\n#### Merge, insertion, and Delete Min\n\n**Merge:** process orders from small to large, considering the two inputs and a possible carry. At most a constant amount of root/link work occurs per order, giving O(log N) total worst-case time. One link is O(1) because descendants are not scanned.\n\n**One link: O(1) | queue merge: O(log N) worst case**\n\n**Insert:** merge with a B0 singleton, carrying through occupied orders until an empty slot is reached. One insertion is O(log N) worst case and O(1) average in the reading. Starting empty, N insertions take O(N) total worst-case time: links combine existing trees and there are fewer than N links overall.\n\n**Insert: O(log N) worst case | N inserts from empty: O(N) total worst case**\n\n**Find Min:** scan the O(log N) roots, unless a correctly maintained minimum reference makes access O(1). **Delete Min:** find/remove the minimum root; its Bk children form a forest B0 through B(k-1). Merge that forest with the other trees. Total worst-case time is O(log N).\n\n**Find Min: O(log N) by root scan, O(1) with cached min | Delete Min: O(log N)**\n\n#### Representation and code meaning\n\nNodes store an element, first-child reference, and next-sibling reference; children are kept in decreasing order. Linking prepends the newly attached, largest-order child. Set its next-sibling to the old first child before replacing the parent's first-child pointer. The smaller root must be the receiving parent.\n\nA destructive queue merge clears the donor's roots and size. For known-node decreaseKey, parent links support upward repair without searching for the node. Page 261 also relates Java PriorityQueue operations such as add, element, and remove to basic priority-queue actions.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Merging is O(log N), not O(1): the work is constant per order, and there are logarithmically many orders. Heap-ordered trees are not fully sorted lists.\n\n### Textbook insertion sequence: 1 through 7\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 16 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Figs. 6.39-6.45, pp. 254-256. Diagram redrawn; carrying steps explained here.*\n\n**Problem.** Begin with an empty binomial queue and insert keys 1,2,3,4,5,6,7 in that order. After every insertion, finish all equal-order links before accepting the next key.\n\n| After inserting | Present orders / roots | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | B0:1 | Create a singleton. |\n| 2 | B1:1 | Link roots 1 and 2; 2 becomes a child. |\n| 3 | B0:3, B1:1 | The B0 slot was empty. |\n| 4 | B2:1 | Link 3 with 4 to get B1:3; link it with B1:1. |\n| 5 | B0:5, B2:1 | No B0 collision. |\n| 6 | B1:5, B2:1 | Link roots 5 and 6; stop at empty B1. |\n| 7 | B0:7, B1:5, B2:1 | No B0 collision. |\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"16\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Binomial queue of seven keys with B0, B1, and B2 trees\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*After inserting 7: B0 at left, B1 in the middle, and B2 at right (Fig. 6.45, p. 256). Total size is 1+2+4=7.*\n\n#### Now insert 8: an original continuation of that example\n\nLink B0 roots 7 and 8 -\u003e B1 rooted at 7. Link that B1 with B1 rooted at 5 -\u003e B2 rooted at 5. Link that B2 with B2 rooted at 1 -\u003e B3 rooted at 1. The result has eight nodes and no other root slot occupied.\n\n#### Delete Min from the seven-node forest instead\n\nRemove root 1 from B2. Its children release B1 rooted at 3 (child 4) and B0 rooted at 2. Merge with existing B1:5 and B0:7: link 2 with 7, leaving three B1 trees; retain one B1 and link two to make B2. Whichever permitted pair is chosen, the result must have B2 and B1, since six is binary 110.\n\n*This last paragraph is an original Delete Min application, not a claim that it is the separate textbook example in Figs. 6.46-6.49.*\n\n### Paper solution for the three-tree carry\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 17 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** Delete Min from the preceding seven-key forest. Show one valid final queue.\n\n| Order | Incoming trees | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | Released 2 and existing 7 | Link under 2 and carry B1 rooted at 2. |\n| 1 | Released 3 (child 4), existing 5 (child 6), carry 2 (child 7) | Keep the carry in B1. Link roots 3 and 5 and carry B2 rooted at 3. |\n| 2 | Carry rooted at 3 only | Store it in B2. |\n\n**Final answer.** Removed 1. B1 is 2 with child 7. B2 is 3 with children 5 and 4, and 5 has child 6. The occupied orders represent 2+4=6 remaining keys.\n\n"},{"id":"fibonacci-pairing-heaps","number":6,"title":"Fibonacci \u0026 Pairing Heaps","page":18,"markdown":"## 06 — Fibonacci \u0026 Pairing Heaps\n\u003ca id=\"fibonacci-pairing-heaps\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: fibonacci-pairing-heaps; topic-number: 06; source-guide-start-page: 18 --\u003e\n### Lazy work, pointer structures, and amortized cost\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 18 --\u003e\n*Jeff Erickson, Efficient Heaps (newfiboheap.pdf), Sections 11.4-11.6, pp. 6-10; runtime table on p. 2.*\n\n#### Lazy binomial / Fibonacci idea\n\nStore a list of heap-ordered root trees and a reference to the minimum. **Insert** appends a singleton; **Merge** splices the two root lists and keeps the smaller minimum. Both use O(1) actual time in the described doubly linked representation because they postpone consolidation.\n\n**Lazy insert and merge: O(1) actual time**\n\n**Extract Min:** remove the minimum root, promote its children to roots, and consolidate equal-degree roots. A degree-indexed array finds partners; each link retains the smaller root and may create another collision at the next degree. Update root parent pointers and the minimum reference.\n\nMany cheap inserts can leave O(N) roots, so one extraction can take O(N) actual time. Charging cleanup work to the operations that created those roots gives O(log N) amortized Extract Min. Amortized bounds concern the total cost of a sequence; they do not make every extraction logarithmic in actual time.\n\n**Lazy Extract Min: O(log N) amortized, O(N) actual worst case**\n\nThe notes' full Fibonacci summary lists O(1) amortized decreaseKey, but its Lazy DecreaseKey subsection is empty. The described cleanup is the lazy-binomial stage of that development; it is not a complete cascading-cut tutorial. Do not assume arbitrary Fibonacci trees keep exact 2^degree sizes after decrease-key operations.\n\n#### Pairing heaps\n\nA pairing heap is one heap-ordered multiway tree without a complete/binomial shape requirement. Nodes keep first-child and next-sibling links. **Link** compares roots and makes the larger-root tree the new first child of the smaller root, taking O(1) actual time. Insert links a singleton.\n\nAfter removing the root, **pair adjacent child heaps from left to right**. If there is an odd final child, carry it unchanged. Then **gather the paired heaps from right to left**. This particular order matters to the amortized guarantee; arbitrary repeated merging is not an equivalent cost argument.\n\nPairing Extract Min can take O(N) actual time when the root has N-1 children; its stated amortized bound is O(log N). For decrease-key with a handle and links supporting direct detachment, do nothing if parent order survives; otherwise cut the subtree and link it back with the remaining heap. Finding an unspecified node is a separate search cost.\n\n**Pairing Extract Min: O(log N) amortized, O(N) actual worst case**\n\nThe supplement discusses the first-child/next-sibling binary view and a potential-based analysis of pairing and gathering. Pointer overhead and memory locality can make theoretically attractive heaps slower in practice. Avoid conflating an operation's immediate pointer work with the best amortized accounting for an entire sequence.\n\n### Original worked examples following the supplemental procedures\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 19 --\u003e\n*Methods: newfiboheap.pdf, pp. 6-9; pairing/gathering illustrated in its Fig. 7, p. 8. Values below are original.*\n\n#### Lazy insertion and cleanup\n\nInsert 3,7,2,9,5 into an empty lazy root list. Before deletion, all five roots may remain separate singletons; minimum reference=2. Extract 2, then consolidate the remaining roots in the listed order:\n\n| Encounter / collision | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Root 3, degree 0 | Place in degree slot 0. |\n| Root 7, degree 0 | Link with 3: root 3 has child 7, degree 1. |\n| Root 9, degree 0 | Place in slot 0. |\n| Root 5, degree 0 | Link with 9: root 5 has child 9, degree 1. |\n| Degree-1 collision: roots 3 and 5 | Link under 3. Final root 3 has children 5 (with child 9) and 7. |\n\nThe exact final sibling order depends on the prescribed link/list convention. Heap order and absence of duplicate consolidated degrees are the invariants to check.\n\n#### Pairing Delete Min\n\nSuppose a removed root has child heaps with roots **8,3,11,7,5**, listed left to right. Pair 8 with 3 -\u003e root 3; pair 11 with 7 -\u003e root 7; carry 5. Gather from the right: link 7 with 5 -\u003e root 5; then link 3 with 5 -\u003e root 3.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"19\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Pairing heap after pairing and right-to-left gathering\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Original result when each winning root prepends the other heap as its first child. There are two first-pass pair links and two gathering links.*\n\n\u003e **STATE THE TYPE OF BOUND**\n\n\u003e A pairing extraction may perform many links in one call. O(log N) amortized describes its sequence-level guarantee and is compatible with this linear actual worst case.\n\n"},{"id":"insertion-sort","number":7,"title":"Insertion Sort","page":20,"markdown":"## 07 — Insertion Sort\n\u003ca id=\"insertion-sort\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: insertion-sort; topic-number: 07; source-guide-start-page: 20 --\u003e\n### Sorted-prefix invariant and shifting\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 20 --\u003e\n*Weiss, p. 272, Fig. 7.1. Stability and storage follow the described shifting procedure.*\n\nInsertion sort performs passes p=1 through N-1, using zero-based positions. Before pass p, positions 0..p-1 are sorted. Save the key at p and insert it into that prefix by shifting larger predecessors one position right.\n\n#### What each pass establishes\n\nAfter pass p, positions **0..p are sorted and contain the same records originally in those positions**. The untouched suffix is not generally sorted. The prefix does not necessarily contain the globally smallest keys; a smaller key may still appear later.\n\nSave the key before shifting because its original cell may be overwritten. Once the hole reaches the start or a predecessor \u0026lt;= the key, write the saved key into the hole. A zero-shift pass is valid: the key already belongs after the prefix's last element.\n\n#### Runtime\n\n**Worst case:** distinct reverse-sorted input makes pass p move past all p predecessors. Total shifts are 1+2+...+(N-1)=N(N-1)/2, giving Theta(N^2) time. **Average:** Theta(N^2) under the reading's input assumptions.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"20\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Reverse-order shifts N(N-1)/2; worst-case Theta(N squared)\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n**Best case:** already sorted input fails the inner shifting test immediately on every pass. N-1 pass checks still remain, giving Theta(N), not constant time. Nearly sorted arrays may also run quickly because few predecessor shifts are necessary.\n\n**Already sorted: Theta(N)**\n\n#### Stability and auxiliary storage\n\nShift only predecessors **strictly greater** than the saved key. Equal earlier records remain before it, making this implementation stable. Using \u003e= instead could reverse equal-key records' order.\n\nThe array is modified in place using one saved key and a few indices, so auxiliary storage is O(1). Shifting a hole avoids the three assignments per swap that repeated exchanging would use.\n\n**Auxiliary space: O(1)**\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e At each pass, insert the next key into the already sorted prefix. Shift larger keys right until the saved key fits. This extends the sorted prefix by one without changing its records or the unprocessed suffix.\n\n### Textbook pass-by-pass example\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 21 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 7.1, p. 272. Input, pass states, and positions moved are reproduced as a study trace.*\n\n**Problem.** Show the array after each pass of increasing insertion sort on 34,8,64,51,32,21.\n\n| Stage | Entire array | Positions moved |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Original | 34, 8, 64, 51, 32, 21 | - |\n| p=1 | 8, 34, 64, 51, 32, 21 | 1 |\n| p=2 | 8, 34, 64, 51, 32, 21 | 0 |\n| p=3 | 8, 34, 51, 64, 32, 21 | 1 |\n| p=4 | 8, 32, 34, 51, 64, 21 | 3 |\n| p=5 | 8, 21, 32, 34, 51, 64 | 4 |\n\n#### Explain two representative passes\n\nAt p=2 the key is 64 and its predecessor 34 is already smaller. There is no shift, but the pass still occurs. At p=4 save key 32, shift 64,51,34 right, then place 32 after 8. Only the original prefix through position 4 changes.\n\n#### An original stability variation\n\nUse labeled records [4a,2,4b], where a originally precedes b. First insert 2 -\u003e [2,4a,4b]. Then insert 4b: predecessor 4a is equal, so the strict-greater test stops immediately. Final equal-key order stays 4a before 4b.\n\nIf the test incorrectly shifted on equality, 4a would move right and 4b would be inserted ahead of it. The array would still be ordered by numeric key, but would no longer be stable.\n\n#### How to write this on a test\n\nWrite the original array and one row after each **completed** pass. Label p explicitly. Include no-movement passes; they demonstrate that the key was checked and the sorted-prefix boundary advanced.\n\n"},{"id":"merge-sort","number":8,"title":"Merge Sort","page":22,"markdown":"## 08 — Merge Sort\n\u003ca id=\"merge-sort\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: merge-sort; topic-number: 08; source-guide-start-page: 22 --\u003e\n### Sorted runs, stable merging, and divide-and-conquer\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 22 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 282-284; the worked merge on pp. 283-284. Later code figures are not imported.*\n\nMergesort divides a positional range into roughly equal halves, recursively sorts both halves, then **merges the two sorted runs**. A range of zero or one element is already sorted. Positional splitting keeps the subproblems balanced independently of key values.\n\n#### The merge operation\n\nKeep an index to the first unprocessed entry of each run and an output index. Compare the two heads; copy the smaller to output and advance **only its input index**, as well as the output index. A sorted run's head is its smallest remaining entry, justifying that choice.\n\nWhen one run empties, append the other run's remaining records directly in order. A merge of N total records takes O(N) time and at most N-1 cross-run key comparisons**, since the remainder needs no further head-to-head comparisons.\n\n**Merge N records: O(N) time and at most N-1 cross-run comparisons**\n\nFor stability, take the **left-run record first on equality**, advancing only the left index. Retain both records: the right equal record will be considered in a later comparison. Stability means preserving equal records' original relative order, not removing duplicates.\n\n#### Recurrence and storage\n\nWith equal halves, T(1)=constant and **T(N)=2T(N/2)+O(N)**. Each complete recursion level processes O(N) records across its subproblems. Halving gives O(log N) levels, hence O(N log N) worst-case time. Standard splitting and merging also continue on already sorted input.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"22\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Merge sort recurrence T(N) equals 2T(N/2)+O(N) and total O(N log N)\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\nArray merging uses O(N) auxiliary array storage. Allocate one temporary array in the public driver and reuse the appropriate portion for each merge; parent merging occurs after child calls finish. The recursive call stack has O(log N) depth and does not change the dominant O(N) auxiliary space.\n\n**Auxiliary array: O(N) | recursive stack: O(log N)**\n\n#### Top-down versus bottom-up practice\n\nThe assigned textbook explanation is recursive: sort the left half, sort the right half, then merge. Your website also has **bottom-up practice**: merge width-1 runs, then width-2 runs, then 4,8,...; process pairs left to right and carry an unpaired run. Width denotes each input run's size, so a width-2 merge normally produces four records.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Each level costs O(N) because the lengths of all its disjoint runs sum to N. There are O(log N) levels because each split halves the range. Multiplying gives O(N log N).\n\n### Textbook eight-key sort and a stable merge\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 23 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 283-284: input 24,13,26,1,2,27,38,15 and final merge. The expanded split table is explanatory.*\n\n| Stage | Runs |\n| --- | --- |\n| Original | [24,13,26,1,2,27,38,15] |\n| Split halves | [24,13,26,1] [2,27,38,15] |\n| Split pairs | [24,13] [26,1] [2,27] [38,15] |\n| Sorted pairs | [13,24] [1,26] [2,27] [15,38] |\n| Sorted halves | [1,13,24,26] [2,15,27,38] |\n| Final merge | [1,2,13,15,24,26,27,38] |\n\n*For an execution trace, recursive order is left child before right child before parent merge. This table groups states by run size for compactness.*\n\n#### The textbook's final merge, one output at a time\n\n| Current heads | Output chosen | What changes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 versus 2 | 1 | Advance left. |\n| 13 versus 2 | 2 | Advance right. |\n| 13 versus 15 | 13 | Advance left. |\n| 24 versus 15 | 15 | Advance right. |\n| 24 versus 27 | 24 | Advance left. |\n| 26 versus 27 | 26 | Left empties; append 27,38. |\n\n#### Original duplicate-key variation\n\nMerge [2a,5] with [2b,4], where the left-run 2a originally preceded 2b. Output 2a first and advance only the left head. Compare 5 with 2b next, then output 2b,4,5. Result [2a,2b,4,5] preserves both equal-key records and their order.\n\n### Paper solution for one bottom-up merge pass\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 24 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** Show the width-2 pass on [1,8 | 2,5 | 3,9]. Each marked run is already sorted.\n\n**Solution.** Merge [1,8] with [2,5]. Compare 1 vs 2, 8 vs 2, then 8 vs 5. Write 1,2,5 in that order and append 8 when the right run empties. Copy the unpaired run [3,9] as a separate run.\n\n**After the pass.** [1,2,5,8 | 3,9]. The bar marks the remaining run boundary. The next pass uses width 4.\n\n"},{"id":"heapsort","number":9,"title":"Heapsort","page":25,"markdown":"## 09 — Heapsort\n\u003ca id=\"heapsort\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: heapsort; topic-number: 09; source-guide-start-page: 25 --\u003e\n### In-place direction, shrinking boundaries, and cost\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 25 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 278-280 and the heapsort analysis on p. 282. Old Quiz 2, Problem 3.*\n\nA naive heap-based sort builds a min-heap, repeatedly removes the minimum, and writes each result into a **second array** in increasing order. The in-place version instead stores each removed extreme in the position released at the end of the shrinking heap.\n\n#### The direction rule from the old quiz\n\nPutting successive minima at the right end yields **decreasing** in-place output. For **increasing** output, reverse the heap-order comparisons and use a **max-heap**. Its largest remaining key goes to the last active position on every iteration.\n\n#### Construction, extraction, restoration\n\nBuild the max-heap bottom-up. In a zero-based array, children of i are 2i+1 and 2i+2; the last non-leaf is floor(N/2)-1. Process that index backward to 0. This takes O(N), not O(N log N).\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"25\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Zero-based heapsort children 2i+1, 2i+2; last internal floor(N/2)-1\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\nThen repeat: **exchange root with last active entry; shrink active size; percolate the replacement down**. Select the larger existing child. Exchange only if that child exceeds the current key. Stop at a leaf or when the current key \u003e= every existing child. A single-child node compares only with its existing child.\n\nThe array has an active heap prefix and a finalized sorted suffix. The exchanged maximum is already in its final position and must not participate in later heap comparisons. Each extraction reduces the prefix by one; N-1 extractions suffice because the final one-element heap is already correctly placed.\n\n#### Complexity and properties\n\nConstruction O(N) plus N-1 extractions of at most O(log N) each gives **O(N log N)** total worst-case time. Standard iterative in-place heapsort uses **O(1) auxiliary storage**: output remains inside the same array. It is **not stable**, because nonadjacent exchanges can reverse equal-key records.\n\n**Build O(N) + N-1 repairs at O(log N) each | total O(N log N)**\n\nThe assigned analysis states average comparisons 2N log2 N - O(N log log N), with a later refinement noted on p. 282. It tracks d_i downward levels during extraction i, with approximately two comparisons per level. Randomness is difficult to analyze because successive extractions change the heap's distribution.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY DIRECTION ANSWER**\n\n\u003e To obtain increasing order in the same array, use a max-heap rather than a min-heap: place each maximum at the last active position, shrink the heap, and restore max-heap order.\n\n### Textbook max-heap and extraction trace\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 26 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 7.6, p. 279; Fig. 7.7, p. 280. Later extraction rows are computed continuations.*\n\n**Problem.** Sort 31,41,59,26,53,58,97 into increasing order in place. The textbook's bottom-up max-heap is shown at left; the heap after its first extraction is at right.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"26\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Heapsort max-heap before and after first extraction\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*After BuildHeap (left) and after exchanging 97 with 31 and restoring six active keys (right). The finalized 97 remains in the array, outside the right-hand tree.*\n\n| Stage | Complete array | Active size |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| After BuildHeap | 97, 53, 59, 26, 41, 58, 31 | 7 |\n| After extraction 1 | 59, 53, 58, 26, 41, 31, 97 | 6 |\n| After extraction 2 | 58, 53, 31, 26, 41, 59, 97 | 5 |\n| After extraction 3 | 53, 41, 31, 26, 58, 59, 97 | 4 |\n| After extraction 4 | 41, 26, 31, 53, 58, 59, 97 | 3 |\n| After extraction 5 | 31, 26, 41, 53, 58, 59, 97 | 2 |\n| After extraction 6 | 26, 31, 41, 53, 58, 59, 97 | 1 |\n\n#### How to explain the first extraction\n\nSwap 97 with final active key 31. Exclude 97 by shrinking the active size to six. At the root, the larger child is 59, so move it upward. The remaining child 58 below the replacement is also larger than 31, so move 58 upward and place 31 in its final hole. The full array is [59,53,58,26,41,31,97].\n\nThis is also how to answer an intermediate-array question: stop after the requested number of **complete** extraction-and-restoration iterations, and show both the active prefix and sorted suffix. A root/end swap alone is not a completed iteration.\n\n### Paper solution for a descending extraction\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 27 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** Starting with the min-heap [1,3,2,7,5,6], show the complete array after one descending heapsort extraction.\n\n| Stage | Active heap prefix | Finalized suffix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Before | [1,3,2,7,5,6] | empty |\n| Swap root and last, then shrink | [6,3,2,7,5] | [1] |\n| Restore using smaller child 2 | [2,3,6,7,5] | [1] |\n\n**Final answer.** [2,3,6,7,5 | 1]. Index 2 has no child within the five-item active prefix. The smallest key is already in the rightmost final position.\n\n"},{"id":"quicksort","number":10,"title":"Quicksort","page":28,"markdown":"## 10 — Quicksort\n\u003ca id=\"quicksort\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: quicksort; topic-number: 10; source-guide-start-page: 28 --\u003e\n### Partition quality, equal keys, and median-of-three setup\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 28 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 288-291 and p. 294; Figs. 7.11-7.12. Website scan conventions are identified separately.*\n\nQuicksort partitions by a chosen key, recursively sorts the smaller and larger groups, and combines their order around the pivot/equal group. Unlike mergesort's positional halves, partition sizes depend on values and the pivot. Average time is **O(N log N)**; repeated highly uneven partitions give **O(N^2)** worst-case time.\n\n**Quicksort time: O(N log N) average, O(N^2) worst case**\n\n#### The simple three-list algorithm\n\nFigure 7.11 chooses the entry at index size/2, forms smaller, same, and larger, recursively sorts only the outer lists, clears the original list, and concatenates smaller/same/larger. All equal records remain in same. A middle-position entry is not necessarily the median value.\n\n#### Choosing a pivot\n\nAlways choosing the first key is bad on sorted or reverse-sorted distinct input: an extreme pivot repeatedly leaves nearly the whole problem on one side. Random selection reduces systematic risk but does not eliminate the worst case. **Median-of-three** chooses the median of the left, center, and right candidates, not the median of the entire subarray.\n\nThe p. 294 setup orders those candidates and parks the pivot at **right-1**. The boundary candidates help act as sentinels. Small subarrays are often handled by insertion sort to reduce overhead; the text discusses small cutoffs, while your website specifically uses a cutoff of three.\n\n#### Partition scans: the convention practiced on your website\n\nThe left scanner stops on a value \u003e= pivot, and the right scanner stops on a value \u0026lt;= pivot. If they stop with i\u0026lt;j, exchange those entries and continue scanning. Once they cross, restore the parked pivot to position i. Recursively sort left..i-1 and i+1..right, excluding that finalized pivot.\n\nBoth scanners stop on equality. Even exchanging equal keys can help the pointers progress and cross near the middle, avoiding an almost-full side on all-equal recursive ranges. Scanning past equality on both sides can produce very uneven partitions.\n\n#### Storage and stability\n\nThe array-partition version avoids the simple algorithm's three output lists, but recursion still uses stack space: O(log N) with balanced partitions and O(N) in the ordinary worst case. Long-distance partition exchanges mean the ordinary in-place version is not stable. These implementation consequences do not mean every quicksort variant has identical storage or stability.\n\n**Stack: O(log N) with balanced partitions, O(N) worst case**\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY WORST-CASE EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Repeated unbalanced partitions give N+(N-1)+... work, so the worst case is quadratic. Median-of-three samples only three entries and therefore reduces risk without guaranteeing balanced halves.\n\n### Textbook pivot example and an explicit median-of-three setup\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 29 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 7.12, p. 291; median-of-three discussion p. 294. Positional practice below is original.*\n\nFigure 7.12 uses the keys {0,13,26,31,43,57,65,75,81,92} and chooses pivot **65**. Its smaller group contains six keys and its larger group contains three. Recursively sorting those groups gives:\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"29\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Sorted textbook pivot grouping\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Textbook key set and pivot, Fig. 7.12. This sorted row summarizes the illustrated grouping; it is not an invented in-place intermediate partition array.*\n\n#### Original positional setup\n\nTake the zero-based array [8,1,9,3,7,2,6]. The left/center/right values are 8,3,6. Order those positions as 3,6,8, giving [3,1,9,6,7,2,8]. Park median 6 at right-1=5 by exchanging positions 3 and 5:\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"29\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Median-of-three setup array and indices\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\nThe left scan passes 1 and stops at 9 (index 2). The right scan stops at 2 (index 3), having passed 7. Exchange 9 and 2 -\u003e [3,1,2,9,7,6,8]. Continuing the strict scans crosses them; restore pivot 6 by exchanging positions 3 and 5:\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"29\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Partitioned array and indices\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\nThe pivot is fixed at 3. Remaining ranges are 0..2 and 4..6. With the website's size-three cutoff, insertion-sort them to get [1,2,3,6,7,8,9]. The two sides need not already be sorted immediately after partitioning.\n\n#### Avoid two common trace mistakes\n\nChoose the middle **value** of the three candidates, not automatically the center value before arranging them. Also distinguish a partition result from the fully sorted recursive result: partitioning separates sides of the pivot but does not finish their internal order.\n\n"},{"id":"sorting-lower-bounds","number":11,"title":"Sorting Lower Bounds","page":30,"markdown":"## 11 — Sorting Lower Bounds\n\u003ca id=\"sorting-lower-bounds\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: sorting-lower-bounds; topic-number: 11; source-guide-start-page: 30 --\u003e\n### Decision trees and the comparison-only limit\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 30 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Section 7.8 on pp. 302-304, Fig. 7.18 and Theorems 7.6-7.7. Separate lecture notes were not supplied.*\n\nA comparison sort learns ordering information by comparing keys. For distinct keys, each comparison has two outcomes, so its behavior can be represented as a binary **decision tree**. An execution follows a root-to-leaf path; its length is the number of comparisons.\n\n#### Why factorially many leaves are necessary\n\nN distinct input records have N! possible relative orderings. At termination, the comparison outcomes must identify a correct sorted arrangement of those records. A leaf cannot stand for two incompatible required orderings. Thus the tree needs at least N! leaves.\n\nA binary tree of maximum depth h has at most 2^h leaves. Therefore **2^h \u003e= N!**, so worst-case comparisons are at least **ceil(log2(N!))**. Because at least about half the factorial's factors are at least N/2, log(N!) is Omega(N log N).\n\n**2^h \u003e= N! | h \u003e= log2(N!) | worst case: Omega(N log N)**\n\n#### What the theorem does and does not establish\n\nEvery general comparison sorter has **Omega(N log N) worst-case** comparisons; with equally likely distinct permutations, average comparisons have the same asymptotic lower bound. This does not mean every input needs that many comparisons: some leaves may be shallower.\n\nA lower bound is a necessary minimum, not an upper bound on every implementation. Insertion sort can still take quadratic time. Mergesort and heapsort have matching O(N log N) worst-case upper bounds, making them asymptotically optimal within this model.\n\nThe proof applies to comparison-based information. It does not prove the same limitation for every method exploiting special key structure. Also, merely drawing a balanced tree with enough leaves is not enough: comparison labels and outcomes must be consistent with realizable key orderings.\n\n#### How to present a written answer\n\nIdentify the N! possibilities, explain the at-most-2^h leaves after h binary comparisons, take a base-two logarithm, and conclude Omega(N log N). Distinguish the exact necessary depth ceil(log2(N!)) from its asymptotic classification.\n\n\u003e **GENERAL INFORMATION PRINCIPLE**\n\n\u003e Distinguishing P possible cases using yes/no questions requires at least ceil(log2 P) questions in the worst case. Sorting substitutes P=N!.\n\n### Worked three-key decision process\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 31 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 7.18, p. 303, illustrates three-key decision trees. The equivalent comparison trace below is original.*\n\n**Problem.** What worst-case comparison lower bound is forced for three distinct keys a,b,c? There are 3!=6 orderings. Depth two permits at most four leaves, while depth three permits eight, so at least one input needs three comparisons.\n\n| First result | Next comparison(s) | Determined increasing order |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a\u003cb | Compare b with c; if b\u003cc, stop. | a,b,c (two comparisons) |\n| a\u003cb and b\u003ec | Compare a with c. | a,c,b if a\u003cc; otherwise c,a,b |\n| a\u003eb | Compare a with c; if a\u003cc, stop. | b,a,c (two comparisons) |\n| a\u003eb and a\u003ec | Compare b with c. | b,c,a if b\u003cc; otherwise c,b,a |\n\nThe tree is allowed to have some two-comparison paths and some three-comparison paths. The **maximum** depth is three, matching the lower bound; saying every execution must use three would be incorrect.\n\n#### Larger exact depth lower bounds\n\n| N | N! possible orderings | Necessary worst-case depth |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 4 | 24 | ceil(log2 24)=5 |\n| 5 | 120 | ceil(log2 120)=7 |\n| 6 | 720 | ceil(log2 720)=10 |\n| 7 | 5040 | ceil(log2 5040)=13 |\n\nThese are information-theoretic necessary bounds, not claims that a simple implementation attains each bound for every N. For example, 2^6=64 is insufficient to distinguish the 120 orderings of five keys, while 2^7=128 is sufficient as a leaf-count capacity.\n\n"},{"id":"external-sorting","number":12,"title":"External Sorting","page":32,"markdown":"## 12 — External Sorting\n\u003ca id=\"external-sorting\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: external-sorting; topic-number: 12; source-guide-start-page: 32 --\u003e\n### Memory-sized runs and sequential merge passes\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 32 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 316-317. Main worked scope: the simple four-tape two-way merge.*\n\nExternal sorting handles a file too large for main memory. Disk/tape input-output can dominate comparisons, so the method uses long sequential accesses instead of assuming cheap arbitrary array access. The textbook's model uses tape pairs, though the run-and-merge idea is more general.\n\n#### Initial run formation\n\nIf memory holds M records, read up to M consecutive records, sort them internally, and write them as one **sorted run**. Alternate these runs between Tb1 and Tb2. The final partial chunk is retained as a shorter run. Initial run count is **ceil(N/M)**.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"32\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Initial runs equals ceiling(N/M)\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n#### A two-way merge pass\n\nRead one run from each input tape and merge them by comparing current heads. Write the resulting run to Ta1, the next pair's run to Ta2, alternating destinations. Within a merge, advance only the input that supplied the selected record. Copy the other run's remainder once one empties.\n\nIf the pass has an unpaired final run, copy it unchanged to the appropriate output tape. Preserve run boundaries: successive runs on one tape need not be sorted relative to each other. Rewind and exchange input/output tape-pair roles for the next pass.\n\n#### Run growth and pass count\n\nA full pair of length-M runs yields a length-2M run; subsequent passes produce nominal lengths 4M,8M,... . With R initial runs, each pass leaves ceil(R/2) runs, so **ceil(log2 R)** additional merge passes suffice. Add one pass if counting initial run construction.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-equation\" data-source-page=\"32\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Next run count ceiling(R/2); merge passes ceiling(log2 R)\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\nSorting ends when one sorted run contains every record, not merely when each tape has internally sorted chunks. The final tape pair depends on the number of role swaps. Merging needs only current records and buffers rather than all N records in memory.\n\n#### Boundary of the assigned reading\n\nThe bottom of p. 317 introduces multiway merging: more input runs can be merged at once when more tapes/buffers are available. The detailed later multiway analysis and replacement-selection technique are outside these assigned pages and are not added here.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY PASS EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Form memory-sized sorted runs, merge pairs sequentially into alternating output tapes, then use those output tapes as the next inputs. Each pass roughly halves the run count until one run remains.\n\n### Textbook tape runs: thirteen records, memory three\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 33 --\u003e\n*Weiss, two-way external-sort example, pp. 316-317. Vertical bars below mark run boundaries, not records.*\n\nAfter initial memory-sized sorting, the textbook has five runs, distributed as follows. They total 13 records, and each initial full run has three records.\n\n| Stage | First tape in output pair | Second tape in output pair |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Initial formation: b tapes | [11,81,94] \\| [17,28,99] \\| [15] | [12,35,96] \\| [41,58,75] |\n| Merge pass 1: a tapes | [11,12,35,81,94,96] \\| [15] | [17,28,41,58,75,99] |\n| Merge pass 2: b tapes | [11,12,17,28,35,41,58,75,81,94,96,99] | [15] |\n| Merge pass 3: a tapes | [11,12,15,17,28,35,41,58,75,81,94,96,99] | empty |\n\n#### Explain the mechanics\n\nPass 1 merges the first run on Tb1 with the first on Tb2, then the second pair. The unpaired [15] is copied as a separate run. In pass 2, the two six-record runs merge while [15] remains unpaired. Pass 3 merges the twelve-record run with [15].\n\nThe run-count progression is **5 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2 -\u003e 1**: three merge passes, plus the initial formation pass. Do not count the unpaired [15] as already globally positioned simply because it is a one-record sorted run.\n\n#### Original pass-count variation\n\nA file of 50 records with memory for six creates ceil(50/6)=9 initial runs. Pairwise passes leave 9 -\u003e 5 -\u003e 3 -\u003e 2 -\u003e 1 runs. That is four additional merge passes, or five passes when initial formation is included.\n\n"},{"id":"graph-fundamentals","number":13,"title":"Graph Fundamentals","page":34,"markdown":"## 13 — Graph Fundamentals\n\u003ca id=\"graph-fundamentals\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: graph-fundamentals; topic-number: 13; source-guide-start-page: 34 --\u003e\n### Definitions, representation, and graph-reading rules\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 34 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 359-362, Figs. 9.1-9.2. Separate assigned Rosen material was not supplied.*\n\nA graph G=(V,E) has vertices V and edges E. A directed edge (u,v) leaves u and enters v; it does not imply the reverse edge. An undirected edge connects both ways. Weights can represent cost, time, distance, or capacity; the application determines their meaning.\n\n#### Paths, cycles, and connectivity\n\nA path listing k consecutive vertices uses k-1 edges. Unweighted length counts those edges; weighted length sums their weights. A vertex has a zero-edge path to itself. A self-loop is an edge from a vertex to itself. A simple path has no repeated vertices, except possibly matching endpoints.\n\nA directed cycle follows edge directions back to its start. In an undirected graph, immediately traversing the same edge out and back does not count as an ordinary cycle because the edge is repeated. A **DAG** is a directed acyclic graph.\n\nAn undirected graph is connected when every vertex pair has a path. A directed graph is **strongly connected** when every vertex can reach every other following directions. If that fails but ignoring directions gives a connected graph, it is **weakly connected**. In a complete undirected simple graph, every pair of distinct vertices is joined; there are V(V-1)/2 edges.\n\n#### Adjacency matrix versus adjacency lists\n\nAn adjacency matrix reserves a cell for every ordered pair, using **Theta(V^2) space**. A directed entry A[u][v] records u-\u003ev. Weighted graphs need a no-edge marker distinct from valid costs: zero weight is not automatically absence. A matrix can be appropriate for dense graphs.\n\n**Adjacency matrix space: Theta(V^2)**\n\nAdjacency lists store each vertex's actual outgoing neighbors and any weights, using **O(V+E) space**. An undirected edge appears once in each endpoint's list, changing only the constant factor. Scanning one outgoing list takes time proportional to that list's length. Lists are usually preferable for sparse graphs.\n\n**Adjacency-list space: O(V+E)**\n\n#### Degree and implementation meaning\n\nA directed vertex's **indegree** counts incoming edges; **outdegree** counts outgoing edges. An adjacency list does not usually list incoming neighbors unless the representation explicitly stores them as well. Named vertices can be mapped to records containing their own neighbor lists and algorithm fields.\n\n\u003e **READ THE GRAPH BEFORE CALCULATING**\n\n\u003e Check directed versus undirected, the meaning of edge weights, and whether zero is a real weight. Reachability may use intermediate vertices, but may not run backward along a directed edge.\n\n### Original graph-to-list and matrix example\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 35 --\u003e\n*Original application of Weiss, pp. 359-362; the layout follows the textbook's vertex/edge style.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"35\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Original directed weighted graph used for adjacency representations\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n**Problem.** Represent exactly the four shown directed edges. A-\u003eC is a real zero-weight edge.\n\n| Vertex | Outgoing weighted adjacency list | Indegree / outdegree |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | B:2, C:0 | 0 / 2 |\n| B | D:5 | 2 / 1 |\n| C | B:1 | 1 / 1 |\n| D | empty | 1 / 0 |\n\n#### Weighted adjacency matrix\n\n| From / to | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A | - | 2 | 0 | - |\n| B | - | - | - | 5 |\n| C | - | 1 | - | - |\n| D | - | - | - | - |\n\n*Here \"-\" means no edge, including no self-loop. A shortest-path distance table can separately initialize a zero distance from a vertex to itself.*\n\nA-\u003eC-\u003eB-\u003eD has three edges and weighted cost 0+1+5=6. It is cheaper than A-\u003eB-\u003eD, which costs 2+5=7 but uses only two edges. D cannot reach A in this directed graph.\n\n"},{"id":"topological-sort-dags","number":14,"title":"Topological Sort / DAGs","page":36,"markdown":"## 14 — Topological Sort / DAGs\n\u003ca id=\"topological-sort-dags\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: topological-sort-dags; topic-number: 14; source-guide-start-page: 36 --\u003e\n### Readiness, indegree updates, and cycle detection\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 36 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 362-364; queue pseudocode Fig. 9.7, p. 365, included under your approved exception.*\n\nA topological ordering is a linear ordering in which every edge u-\u003ev places u before v. It exists exactly for DAGs. The ordering need not be unique: vertices with no dependency relationship may be interleaved differently.\n\n#### Indegree-based algorithm\n\nCompute every initial indegree by scanning edges. Put all zero-indegree vertices into a ready queue. Repeatedly remove one ready vertex, append it to the order, and decrement the indegree of **each outgoing neighbor**. If a decrement makes a neighbor's count zero, add it to the queue.\n\nCurrent indegree counts remaining prerequisites among unprocessed vertices. Removing a vertex conceptually removes its outgoing edges, so its dependents become closer to readiness. Incoming counts do not increase, and an unselected ready vertex stays ready.\n\n#### What a stall means\n\nIf the ready queue becomes empty before all V vertices are processed, the remaining graph has a directed cycle. A nonempty finite DAG would have at least one zero-indegree vertex. The counter in the pseudocode therefore detects a cycle when fewer than V vertices receive an order position.\n\nA directed cycle prevents a valid order because its constraints ultimately require a vertex to precede itself. An isolated vertex is initially eligible and can appear anywhere that does not disrupt the other constraints.\n\n#### Runtime and selection conventions\n\nRepeatedly scanning all vertices for a new zero-indegree vertex can cost O(V^2). A ready queue avoids that search. Initializing counters and processing vertices costs O(V); each edge is decremented once when its source is processed, giving **O(V+E)** total with adjacency lists.\n\n**Topological sort: O(V+E) with a ready queue and adjacency lists**\n\nFIFO order is one valid tie convention. A stack or another rule choosing among currently ready vertices can also produce a valid topological order. If an exercise specifies alphabetic/FIFO choices, follow them to obtain its requested trace.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY CYCLE ANSWER**\n\n\u003e If vertices remain but no remaining vertex has indegree zero, the remaining directed graph contains a cycle. It cannot supply the next vertex of a complete topological order.\n\n### Original queue trace with two initial choices\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 37 --\u003e\n*Original example following Weiss, Figs. 9.5-9.7, pp. 364-365.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"37\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Four-vertex DAG for topological-order example\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n**Problem.** Produce a topological order for A-\u003eC, B-\u003eC, C-\u003eD. Initialize the ready queue alphabetically and use FIFO order thereafter.\n\n| Step | Queue before removal | Output | Indegree change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Initial | A,B | empty | A=0, B=0, C=2, D=1 |\n| Remove A | A,B | A | C: 2 -\u003e 1; not ready yet |\n| Remove B | B | A,B | C: 1 -\u003e 0; enqueue C |\n| Remove C | C | A,B,C | D: 1 -\u003e 0; enqueue D |\n| Remove D | D | A,B,C,D | No outgoing edges |\n\nB,A,C,D is also mathematically valid, but it uses a different choice among the initial ready vertices. Neither C,A,B,D nor A,C,B,D works because C must follow both A and B.\n\n#### Original cycle variation\n\nAdd edge D-\u003eB. Only A initially has indegree zero. Processing A reduces C from 2 to 1, but the queue then empties. B-\u003eC-\u003eD-\u003eB is a directed cycle, so there is no complete topological order.\n\nWhen explaining a result, check each edge's endpoints in the proposed order. Drawing positions or alphabetical order do not override dependency arrows.\n\n"},{"id":"dijkstras-algorithm","number":15,"title":"Dijkstra's Algorithm","page":38,"markdown":"## 15 — Dijkstra's Algorithm\n\u003ca id=\"dijkstras-algorithm\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: dijkstras-algorithm; topic-number: 15; source-guide-start-page: 38 --\u003e\n### Greedy finalization for nonnegative weighted edges\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 38 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 372-377, especially Figs. 9.20-9.28. Old Quiz 2, Problem 1 informs algorithm-selection review.*\n\nDijkstra computes shortest total-weight paths from a source when **all edge weights are nonnegative**. Zero-weight edges are allowed. It does not minimize the number of edges unless every edge has the same weight, and it does not optimize the total edge weight of a spanning tree.\n\n#### Table fields and initialization\n\nMaintain a tentative distance, predecessor, and known/finalized flag per vertex. Set the source distance to 0 and all others to infinity; initially all are unknown. A tentative distance is the best route discovered using the processed vertices as intermediates.\n\n#### One iteration\n\nSelect an unknown vertex v with the smallest **finite** tentative distance; declare it known. For each eligible outgoing neighbor w, form candidate dist[v]+weight(v,w). If it is strictly smaller than dist[w], update the distance and record v as predecessor. An equal candidate need not replace the existing predecessor.\n\n**Select smallest finite unknown dist[v] | candidate = dist[v] + weight(v,w)**\n\nRepeat until no unknown vertex has a finite tentative distance. Remaining vertices are unreachable from the source and retain infinity. When ties occur, either minimum is safe; a specified tie rule controls the exact predecessor trace.\n\n#### Why the greedy step is safe\n\nA later alternative route must pass through another unknown vertex before reaching the selected one. Its distance cannot be below the current smallest tentative distance, and nonnegative remaining edges cannot reduce that cost. Thus the selected minimum cannot later improve.\n\nA negative edge invalidates this argument: a more distant unknown vertex may later provide a cheaper return to an already finalized vertex. A negative edge need not form a negative cycle, but standard Dijkstra is not generally valid for such a graph.\n\n#### Paths, priority operations, and runtime\n\nRecover a route by following predecessor pointers backward from destination to source and reversing the result. The source has no predecessor. Selecting the least tentative vertex corresponds to Delete Min; improving a queued distance corresponds conceptually to Decrease Key.\n\nAn array-scan implementation selects among V records up to V times, giving **O(V^2)** overall for an ordinary dense graph. Priority queues improve selection for sparse graphs; the assigned pages introduce this motivation. Keep the scan implementation separate from a particular later heap-based implementation.\n\n**Dijkstra with array scan: O(V^2)**\n\n\u003e **APPLICATION DISTINCTION**\n\n\u003e A shortest route for each driver does not address shared road capacity. If the question asks how to route a crowd without exceeding capacities, identify the throughput/flow objective rather than assuming Dijkstra is the right model.\n\n### Textbook seven-vertex example from source v1\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 39 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 9.20, p. 374; table evolution Figs. 9.21-9.27, pp. 374-376; stages in Fig. 9.28, p. 377.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"39\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Directed seven-vertex graph for Dijkstra textbook example\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Textbook edges and weights, redrawn. Arrows are essential; for example v3-\u003ev1 does not supply a direct v1-\u003ev3 route.*\n\n| After finalizing | d1 | d2 | d3 | d4 | d5 | d6 | d7 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| v1 | 0 | 2 | inf | 1 | inf | inf | inf |\n| v4 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 9 | 5 |\n| v2 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 9 | 5 |\n| v5 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 9 | 5 |\n| v3 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 8 | 5 |\n| v7 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 6 | 5 |\n| v6 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 3 | 6 | 5 |\n\nThe v3/v5 tie at distance 3 is resolved as in the textbook by taking v5 before v3. Final predecessors are: v2\u0026lt;-v1, v3\u0026lt;-v4, v4\u0026lt;-v1, v5\u0026lt;-v4, v6\u0026lt;-v7, v7\u0026lt;-v4.\n\nFor v6, route v1-\u003ev4-\u003ev7-\u003ev6 costs 1+4+1=6. Its initial candidate via v4 was 1+8=9; processing v3 improved it to 3+5=8, and processing v7 improved it again to 6 before finalization.\n\n### Paper solution for one Dijkstra iteration\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 40 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** In the preceding textbook trace, finalize v4 immediately after v1 and show all outgoing relaxations.\n\n**Solution.** Select v4 because 1 is the smallest finite unknown distance. Mark v4 known. Its predecessor remains v1.\n\n| Target | Old distance | Candidate via v4 | New distance / predecessor |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| v3 | infinity | 1+2=3 | 3 / v4 |\n| v5 | infinity | 1+2=3 | 3 / v4 |\n| v6 | infinity | 1+8=9 | 9 / v4 |\n| v7 | infinity | 1+4=5 | 5 / v4 |\n\nAll other records stay as they were. Next, v2 has the smallest unknown distance, 2.\n\n#### Paper explanation for a negative-edge failure\n\nLet S-\u003eA cost 2, S-\u003eB cost 5, and B-\u003eA cost -4. Dijkstra finalizes A at 2 before B at 5. The route S-\u003eB-\u003eA costs 5-4=1, so A was finalized too early. This directed graph is acyclic.\n\n"},{"id":"shortest-path-algorithms","number":16,"title":"Shortest-Path Algorithms","page":41,"markdown":"## 16 — Shortest-Path Algorithms\n\u003ca id=\"shortest-path-algorithms\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: shortest-path-algorithms; topic-number: 16; source-guide-start-page: 41 --\u003e\n### Choose the method from the weight and graph restrictions\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 41 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 366-370, p. 372, pp. 380-381; related all-pairs and word-ladder remarks on p. 384.*\n\n#### Unweighted shortest paths: BFS\n\nTreat every edge as weight one. Initialize source distance 0 and all others infinity, then enqueue the source. Dequeue v and discover each still-infinite outgoing neighbor w with dist[w]=dist[v]+1 and predecessor v; enqueue w once. FIFO order processes earlier distance layers before later ones, so first discovery gives the shortest edge count.\n\nWith adjacency lists, each vertex and edge is processed O(1) times, giving **O(V+E)**. The initial repeated-layer scanning version discussed before the queue improvement can take O(V^2). Infinity/undiscovered checks prevent duplicate first-discovery enqueues.\n\n**BFS: O(V+E) with adjacency lists**\n\n#### Negative edge costs: allow later correction\n\nThe queue-based negative-edge method does not permanently finalize vertices. Dequeue v, relax outgoing edges with dist[v]+cost(v,w), and enqueue an improved neighbor if it is not already queued. Its pending processing can use a newly improved distance without adding a duplicate queue entry.\n\nWithout negative-cost cycles, the stated worst-case time is **O(VE)**. A source-reachable negative cycle can cause indefinitely repeated improvements in the unguarded algorithm. Destinations reachable from such a cycle have no finite lower bound on path cost. The text discusses counting excessive dequeues to force termination/detect the problem.\n\n**Queue-based correction: O(VE) worst case without negative-cost cycles**\n\nAdding a constant to every edge does not safely remove negative weights: a k-edge path gains k times that constant, so routes with different edge counts can reverse their cost order.\n\n#### Weighted DAGs: topological relaxation\n\nProcess vertices in topological order and relax outgoing edges. All incoming predecessors are already processed when a vertex is reached, so its value cannot later improve from a backward dependency. This works with negative edges too, and takes **O(V+E)** including the ordering.\n\n**DAG shortest paths: O(V+E), including topological ordering**\n\n#### Related boundary-page applications\n\nPage 384 notes all-pairs shortest paths: one can run a single-source algorithm from every vertex; a specialized O(V^3) method is discussed later, not derived in these assigned pages. It also models a **word ladder** as an unweighted graph: words are vertices and one-character transformations are edges. BFS then finds a fewest-transformation ladder.\n\n\u003e **METHOD SELECTION**\n\n\u003e Unweighted -\u003e BFS. Nonnegative weighted -\u003e Dijkstra. Weighted DAG, even with negative edges -\u003e topological relaxation. General negative edges without relevant negative cycles -\u003e a correction-based method, not permanent Dijkstra finalization.\n\n### Three original examples that distinguish the cases\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 42 --\u003e\n*Original applications of the BFS and relaxation procedures in Weiss, pp. 366-372, 380-381.*\n\n#### A. Unweighted BFS layers\n\nEdges: S-\u003eA, S-\u003eB, A-\u003eC, B-\u003eD, C-\u003eT, D-\u003eT. Starting at S, the layers are {S}, {A,B}, {C,D}, {T}. Thus dist(T)=3 edges. With A processed before B and C before D, T's recorded predecessor is C; the route through D is equally short.\n\n| Distance | Newly discovered vertices | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | S | Source initialization |\n| 1 | A,B | One edge from S |\n| 2 | C,D | One edge from a distance-1 vertex |\n| 3 | T | One edge from a distance-2 vertex |\n\n#### B. Why adding a constant changes the answer\n\nSuppose edges are S-\u003eA(2), A-\u003eT(-3), and S-\u003eT(0). The two-edge route costs -1, beating direct cost 0. Add 3 to every edge: the two-edge route now costs 5+0=5, while the direct route costs 3. The transformation incorrectly makes the direct path appear best.\n\nThe original graph is a DAG, so topological relaxation S,A,T correctly gives dist(T)=-1 despite the negative edge. It needs no nonnegative-edge greedy assumption.\n\n#### C. A reachable negative cycle\n\nEdges S-\u003eA(1), A-\u003eB(-4), B-\u003eA(1), B-\u003eT(2) give the cycle A-\u003eB-\u003eA a total of -3. The source can reach the cycle and the cycle can reach T. Each extra cycle traversal lowers the route to T by another three, so T has **no finite shortest cost**.\n\nA negative cycle elsewhere, unreachable from S or unable to reach a particular destination, does not by itself make that destination's source distance unbounded below. Check both reachability conditions.\n\n### Paper solution for a queue correction\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 43 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** The outgoing edges of S are processed A first, then B. Edges are S-\u003eA(2), S-\u003eB(5), B-\u003eA(-4), A-\u003eT(2). Show how the queue method fixes a previously processed distance.\n\n| Processed | Successful changes | Queue afterward |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| S | A=2 via S, B=5 via S | [A,B] |\n| A | T=4 via A | [B,T] |\n| B | A=1 via B, so enqueue A again | [T,A] |\n| T | None | [A] |\n| A | T=3 via A, so enqueue T again | [T] |\n| T | None | empty |\n\n**Final answer.** T has distance 3 on S-\u003eB-\u003eA-\u003eT. Both A and T needed reprocessing.\n\n"},{"id":"activity-graphs","number":17,"title":"Activity Graphs","page":44,"markdown":"## 17 — Activity Graphs\n\u003ca id=\"activity-graphs\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: activity-graphs; topic-number: 17; source-guide-start-page: 44 --\u003e\n### Tasks, events, precedence, and parallel work\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 44 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 380-384, particularly Figs. 9.33-9.34, p. 382.*\n\nAn **activity-node graph** puts a task and its duration at each vertex. An edge A-\u003eB means A must finish before B can begin. Dependencies can be transitive: A-\u003eB-\u003eC also requires A before C, even without a direct A-\u003eC edge.\n\n#### Precedence is not the same as execution order\n\nTasks without a dependency relationship may run concurrently if sufficient independent servers/resources exist. A topological ordering respects dependencies, but does not require unrelated tasks to run one at a time. Real resource limits may impose additional delays beyond this precedence-only model.\n\nThe graph must be acyclic; a cycle creates circular prerequisites. If C requires both A and B, C starts only after both finish, at the **maximum** of their completion times. Finishing the shorter branch does not bypass the other requirement.\n\n#### Activity-node versus event-node representation\n\nAn **event-node graph** puts milestones at vertices and activities/durations on edges. Conversion must preserve the intended prerequisite relationships. Zero-duration **dummy edges** can encode dependencies without adding real work. Zero duration does not mean zero logical significance or necessarily zero slack.\n\nA bad conversion can add a false dependency, making a task wait for unnecessary work, or omit a required dependency, allowing it to start too early. When two activities have different prerequisite sets, merging their start events carelessly can create this error.\n\n#### Project duration is a longest-chain constraint\n\nAdding every duration assumes all tasks are serial, which overestimates completion when independent work overlaps. In a DAG with sufficient resources, the **longest required start-to-finish duration chain** determines earliest project completion. A shortest chain ignores other tasks that must also finish.\n\nThe next section's forward/backward event calculations identify earliest finish, allowable delays, slack, and critical activities. They use a topological order, and should not be confused with Dijkstra's minimum-distance objective.\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY CONVERSION EXPLANATION**\n\n\u003e Use dummy zero-duration edges where necessary to preserve exactly the original dependencies. They add ordering constraints without adding execution time.\n\n### Textbook project: independent tasks and the critical chain\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 45 --\u003e\n*Weiss, activity-node graph Fig. 9.33, p. 382; discussion on pp. 381-382. Redrawn without the start/finish arrows.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"45\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Textbook activity-node dependency graph\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*A/3 means activity A takes three time units. A and B can start at time zero; H is the final activity. Node positions are adjusted for readability.*\n\n| Activity | Prerequisites | Earliest start | Earliest finish |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A (3) | none | 0 | 3 |\n| B (2) | none | 0 | 2 |\n| C (3) | A | 3 | 6 |\n| D (2) | A,B | max(3,2)=3 | 5 |\n| E (1) | B | 2 | 3 |\n| F (3) | C,D | max(6,5)=6 | 9 |\n| G (2) | D,E | max(5,3)=5 | 7 |\n| K (4) | E | 3 | 7 |\n| H (1) | F,G,K | max(9,7,7)=9 | 10 |\n\nThe critical chain **A,C,F,H** takes 3+3+3+1=10. B may finish as late as time 4 without delaying D or the overall finish, whereas delaying A,C,F, or H without compensation delays the project.\n\nA conversion must let C depend only on A, and E depend only on B, while D requires both. Those differing prerequisite sets explain the dummy connections in the event-node graph (Fig. 9.34).\n\n### Paper solution for a dummy dependency\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 46 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** A and B can start together. C requires only A, E requires only B, and D requires both. Show how to draw this in an event-node network.\n\n**Solution.** Give A and B separate finish events 2 and 3. Draw C out of 2 and E out of 3. Add a zero-duration arrow from 2 to a new join event J and another from 3 to J. Draw D out of J.\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"46\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Event-node graph with dummy dependencies\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n**Justification.** J waits for both finish events, so D has both prerequisites. C can start when A finishes, and E can start when B finishes. The dummy arrows add zero time.\n\n"},{"id":"pert-critical-path","number":18,"title":"PERT / Critical-Path Timing","page":47,"markdown":"## 18 — PERT / Critical-Path Timing\n\u003ca id=\"pert-critical-path\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: pert-critical-path; topic-number: 18; source-guide-start-page: 47 --\u003e\n### Forward maxima, backward minima, and slack\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 47 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 381-384, Figs. 9.34-9.37. Separate PERT lecture notes and a specific Wikipedia reading were not supplied.*\n\nUse an acyclic **event-node network**: an activity edge v-\u003ew has duration c(v,w), while each event receives an earliest completion EC and a latest completion LC. These event times differ from activity durations.\n\n#### Forward pass: earliest event times\n\nSet the initial event EC=0. Process events in **topological order**. For each event w, compute **EC(w)=max over incoming v of [EC(v)+c(v,w)]**. The maximum is required because every incoming prerequisite must have finished. The final event's EC is the earliest possible project finish.\n\n**EC(start) = 0 | EC(w) = max over incoming v of [EC(v) + c(v,w)]**\n\n#### Backward pass: latest event times\n\nTo preserve that earliest finish, set **LC(final)=EC(final)**. Process in reverse topological order and compute **LC(v)=min over outgoing w of [LC(w)-c(v,w)]**. Every successor places a latest allowable bound, and the minimum satisfies all of them. A different specified finish deadline changes the latest times and slack.\n\n**LC(final) = EC(final) | LC(v) = min over outgoing w of [LC(w) - c(v,w)]**\n\n#### Slack and critical paths\n\nFor activity v-\u003ew, **total slack=LC(w)-EC(v)-c(v,w)**. It is not merely LC(v)-EC(v), which describes event flexibility. Slack zero identifies a critical activity for the specified finish. A start-to-finish chain of zero-slack activities is a critical path; multiple critical paths may tie.\n\n**Activity slack(v,w) = LC(w) - EC(v) - c(v,w) | critical if slack = 0**\n\nA delay to a critical activity delays completion unless other schedule changes compensate. Positive total slack allows delay relative to the earliest schedule, but slack allowances on linked activities may overlap; they are not necessarily independent budgets to spend simultaneously.\n\n#### Runtime and exam explanation\n\nWith the necessary incoming/outgoing adjacency information, topological ordering and both passes take **O(V+E)**. Longest-path timing is efficient here because the network is acyclic. Zero-duration dummy edges still impose precedence and participate in these constraints.\n\n**PERT timing passes and topological ordering: O(V+E)**\n\nThe old quiz supplies earliest event times and asks for latest times and an explanation. Begin from the final event's required finish, work backward, subtract each outgoing activity duration from its successor's LC, and take the minimum. If EC values are already given, you need not recompute the forward pass merely to find LC.\n\n\u003e **SOURCE SCOPE**\n\n\u003e This section summarizes the verified deterministic textbook event-network treatment. It does not invent missing lecture content or attribute a three-estimate probabilistic PERT formula to the supplied pages.\n\n### Textbook event graph and completed timing table\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 48 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 9.34, p. 382; EC in Fig. 9.35 and LC in Fig. 9.36, p. 383. Redrawn with a compact table.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"48\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Textbook PERT event network with dummy edges\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Activity labels give name/duration; edges labeled 0 are dummies. Primed events separate dependencies without introducing extra work.*\n\n| Event | EC | LC | LC-EC |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 2 | 3 | 3 | 0 |\n| 3 | 2 | 4 | 2 |\n| 6' | 3 | 4 | 1 |\n| 4 | 6 | 6 | 0 |\n| 5 | 3 | 5 | 2 |\n| 6 | 5 | 6 | 1 |\n| 7' | 6 | 6 | 0 |\n| 8' | 5 | 7 | 2 |\n| 9 | 7 | 9 | 2 |\n| 7 | 9 | 9 | 0 |\n| 8 | 7 | 9 | 2 |\n| 10' | 9 | 9 | 0 |\n| 10 | 10 | 10 | 0 |\n\nExample forward step: EC(7')=max(EC(4),EC(6))=max(6,5)=6. Activity F then finishes at EC(7)=6+3=9. The finish event has EC(10)=9+1=10.\n\n### Backward calculations and activity slack\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 49 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Figs. 9.36-9.37, pp. 383-384; same textbook event network as the preceding page.*\n\n#### Show the backward-pass arithmetic\n\nSet LC(10)=10. Since H takes one unit, LC(10')=10-1=9. Its zero-duration incoming branches give LC(7)=LC(8)=LC(9)=9. Going backward through F gives LC(7')=9-3=6; through G gives LC(8')=9-2=7.\n\nEvent 6 has zero-duration edges to 7' and 8', so **LC(6)=min(6,7)=6**. Event 5 has edges to 8' (duration 0) and 9 (K of duration 4), so **LC(5)=min(7-0,9-4)=5**. The minimum prevents either successor from being delayed.\n\n| Activity | EC(tail) | LC(head) | Duration | Total slack |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 0 | 3 | 3 | 0 |\n| B | 0 | 4 | 2 | 2 |\n| C | 3 | 6 | 3 | 0 |\n| D | 3 | 6 | 2 | 1 |\n| E | 2 | 5 | 1 | 2 |\n| F | 6 | 9 | 3 | 0 |\n| G | 5 | 9 | 2 | 2 |\n| K | 3 | 9 | 4 | 2 |\n| H | 9 | 10 | 1 | 0 |\n\nFor D, slack=LC(6)-EC(6')-2=6-3-2=1. For B, slack=LC(3)-EC(1)-2=4-0-2=2. Critical activities **A,C,F,H** have zero slack and form the ten-unit critical path.\n\n#### Original two-constraint check\n\nIf an event V has outgoing activities V-\u003eX of duration 4 and V-\u003eY of duration 3, with LC(X)=12 and LC(Y)=15, then LC(V)=min(12-4,15-3)=min(8,12)=8. Choosing 12 would make the X activity finish at 16, violating LC(X)=12.\n\n\u003e **WHAT TO WRITE ON THE TEST**\n\n\u003e State the final-time initialization, reverse topological order, and minimum-of-successor-deadlines-minus-duration rule. Then show the arithmetic for each event rather than listing unexplained numbers.\n\n"},{"id":"network-flow","number":19,"title":"Network Flow","page":50,"markdown":"## 19 — Network Flow\n\u003ca id=\"network-flow\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: network-flow; topic-number: 19; source-guide-start-page: 50 --\u003e\n### Paper procedure and residual ledger\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 50 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**On paper.** Start every original edge at flow 0. Write its capacity c and current flow f. For original u-\u003ev, the forward residual u-\u003ev is c-f and the reverse residual v-\u003eu is f. Omit zero residual arcs when searching.\n\n**Forward residual = c - f | reverse residual = f**\n\n**Repeat.** Find an s-to-t residual path, then write its residual capacities in order. Take their minimum as the bottleneck. Add that amount on forward original edges and subtract it on any original edge used backward. Increase total flow by the bottleneck. Recompute both residual entries for each changed edge.\n\n**Forward arc: f increases by bottleneck | reverse arc: f decreases by bottleneck**\n\n**Textbook example ledger.** The next pages show the original diagram and cut proof. Each state below lists (flow, forward residual, reverse residual).\n\n| Original edge | Capacity | Initial | After s,a,d,t | After s,b,d,a,c,t |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| s-\u003ea | 4 | (0,4,0) | (3,1,3) | (3,1,3) |\n| s-\u003eb | 2 | (0,2,0) | (0,2,0) | (2,0,2) |\n| a-\u003eb | 1 | (0,1,0) | (0,1,0) | (0,1,0) |\n| a-\u003ec | 2 | (0,2,0) | (0,2,0) | (2,0,2) |\n| a-\u003ed | 4 | (0,4,0) | (3,1,3) | (1,3,1) |\n| b-\u003ed | 2 | (0,2,0) | (0,2,0) | (2,0,2) |\n| c-\u003et | 3 | (0,3,0) | (0,3,0) | (2,1,2) |\n| d-\u003et | 3 | (0,3,0) | (3,0,3) | (3,0,3) |\n| Residual path | — | Residual capacities | Bottleneck | Total flow |\n| s,a,d,t | — | 4,4,3 | 3 | 0+3=3 |\n| s,b,d,a,c,t | — | 2,2,3,2,3 | 2 | 3+2=5 |\n\n**Reverse step to show.** Using d-\u003ea with bottleneck 2 changes original a-\u003ed from flow 3 to 1. Its forward residual rises from 1 to 3 and its reverse residual falls from 3 to 1.\n\n**Stop and certify.** Search from s through all positive residual arcs. If t is unreachable, put the reachable vertices in S. Sum original capacities directed from S to its complement. A feasible flow matching that sum is maximum.\n\n**Stop when t is unreachable in the residual graph | flow value = cut capacity**\n\n### Feasible throughput, residual choices, and optimality\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 51 --\u003e\n*Weiss, pp. 386-392, Figs. 9.39-9.49. Old Quiz 2, Problem 1 motivates the capacity-versus-distance distinction.*\n\nMaximum flow asks for the largest feasible throughput from source s to sink t. Each directed edge has capacity c and assigned flow f satisfying 0\u0026lt;=f\u0026lt;=c. At every **internal vertex**, total inflow equals total outflow. The source supplies net flow and the sink receives it.\n\n#### Flow graph and residual graph\n\nThe flow graph Gf records current assignments. The residual graph Gr records possible changes: an original edge with capacity c and flow f has **forward residual capacity c-f** and a **reverse cancellation capacity f**. If both original directions exist, bookkeeping must distinguish their contributions.\n\n**Forward residual = c - f | reverse residual = f**\n\nA positive-residual s-to-t path is an **augmenting path**. Its bottleneck is the minimum residual capacity along it. Add that amount to forward-used original edges and subtract it from original edges traversed via reverse cancellation arcs. The net source-to-sink flow increases by the bottleneck.\n\n**Bottleneck = min(path residual capacities) | new total = old total + bottleneck**\n\n#### Why reverse edges are essential\n\nAn early route can use capacity needed by a better combination of later routes. Reverse residual arcs let the algorithm change its mind. A search restricted to unused forward original capacity can get stuck below the maximum.\n\n#### Cuts and the stopping certificate\n\nA cut separates vertices into S containing the source and T containing the sink. Its directed capacity sums **original capacities from S to T**. Every feasible flow value is \u0026lt;= every cut capacity. The maximum-flow value equals the minimum-cut capacity.\n\nWhen no residual s-to-t path remains, let S be the vertices still reachable from s. The boundary gives a cut with capacity equal to the current flow, proving optimality. Reaching a dead end on one chosen path is not enough; the whole residual graph must lack an augmenting route.\n\n#### Runtime and path selection\n\nFor integer capacities, each positive augmentation increases flow by at least one, so arbitrary augmenting paths give O(fE) for maximum value f. Large numerical capacities can make that expensive. Page 392 discusses greatest-bottleneck paths and fewest-edge residual paths; BFS finds the latter. The later continuation of the BFS complexity proof is outside this flow page range.\n\n**Arbitrary integral augmentations: O(FE), where F is maximum flow value**\n\n\u003e **EXAM-READY APPLICATION ANSWER**\n\n\u003e Use flow when many routes share limited capacities and the objective is total throughput. Selecting a shortest path for each attendee independently does not account for those capacity interactions.\n\n### Textbook network: a bad first choice can be repaired\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 52 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 9.39, p. 387; correct residual repair in Figs. 9.46-9.47, pp. 390-391.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"52\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Textbook network-flow capacity graph\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Textbook capacities, redrawn. In particular a-\u003ed has capacity 4, while both c-\u003et and d-\u003et have capacity 3.*\n\n#### Start by sending three units through s,a,d,t\n\nThis uses all d-\u003et capacity and three of the four a-\u003ed units. Flow value is 3. A forward-only search cannot use s,b,d,t because d-\u003et is saturated, but the correct residual graph also contains **d-\u003ea with capacity 3**, permitting cancellation.\n\n#### Augment two units along s,b,d,a,c,t\n\nResidual capacities along this path are 2,2,3,2,3, so the bottleneck is 2. The d-\u003ea arc is reverse: reduce original a-\u003ed flow from 3 to 1. Increase s-\u003eb, b-\u003ed, a-\u003ec, and c-\u003et by 2. Total flow becomes 5.\n\n| Original edge | Final flow / capacity |\n| --- | --- |\n| s-\u003ea, s-\u003eb | 3/4, 2/2 |\n| a-\u003eb, a-\u003ec, a-\u003ed | 0/1, 2/2, 1/4 |\n| b-\u003ed, c-\u003et, d-\u003et | 2/2, 2/3, 3/3 |\n\nCheck conservation at a: inflow 3 equals outgoing 2+1. At d: inflow 1+2 equals outgoing 3. Reverse residual use has rerouted earlier flow without violating original capacities.\n\n### A matching cut and the augmentation-cost pitfall\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 53 --\u003e\n*Weiss, cut in Fig. 9.40, p. 387; reachable-cut principle Fig. 9.48, p. 391; bad-case Fig. 9.49, p. 392.*\n\n#### Certify the textbook maximum of five\n\nFor the final flow, start the residual search at s. Follow s-\u003ea (1), a-\u003eb (1), and a-\u003ed (3). Every positive residual arc out of these reached vertices stays within S={s,a,b,d}. In particular, a-\u003ec and d-\u003et both have forward residual 0. Thus t is unreachable and T={c,t}. The original capacities across S to T are a-\u003ec (2) and d-\u003et (3), so the cut capacity is 2+3=5.\n\nWe have a feasible flow of value 5 and an upper bound of 5 from a cut. Therefore the flow is maximum. It is insufficient merely to point at source outgoing capacity 4+2=6 or sink incoming capacity 3+3=6; a smaller downstream cut is the binding constraint.\n\n| Claim to check | Evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| Feasibility | Every original edge flow is within capacity; internal inflow equals outflow. |\n| Value | Net source outflow is 3+2=5, matching sink inflow 2+3. |\n| Optimality | The directed cut {s,a,b,d} / {c,t} has capacity 5. |\n\n#### Why a small graph can take many arbitrary augmentations\n\nThe textbook's Fig. 9.49 gives two million-capacity branches with a connecting edge of capacity one. Favoring paths that repeatedly use/cancel that narrow connection may increase total flow by just one per step, requiring about two million augmentations instead of sending a million down each side in two steps.\n\nThe O(fE) bound depends on flow value f, not just the number of vertices and edges. Choosing a greatest-bottleneck path or a fewest-edge residual path is intended to avoid this behavior. Reverse edges are still required regardless of the selection rule.\n\n\u003e **ONE PATH UPDATE CHECKLIST**\n\n\u003e Find the minimum positive residual capacity, add on forward arcs, subtract on reverse arcs, and update residual capacities in both directions. Then verify conservation and continue searching the full residual network.\n\n"},{"id":"prims-algorithm","number":20,"title":"Prim's Algorithm","page":54,"markdown":"## 20 — Prim's Algorithm\n\u003ca id=\"prims-algorithm\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- topic-id: prims-algorithm; topic-number: 20; source-guide-start-page: 54 --\u003e\n### Grow one minimum spanning tree\n\u003c!-- section-role: concepts; source-guide-page: 54 --\u003e\n*Weiss, MST/Prim portions of pp. 393-397, Figs. 9.50-9.57. Old Quiz 2, Problem 4 explicitly requires Prim.*\n\nA spanning tree connects all vertices of a connected undirected graph without cycles and has **V-1 edges**. A **minimum spanning tree (MST)** minimizes the sum of those selected edge weights. It is not a shortest-path tree from one source.\n\n#### The growing-tree rule\n\nStart with one chosen vertex and no edges. At each step choose a minimum-weight edge with **one endpoint in the current tree and one outside**. Add that edge and its outside vertex. An internal edge would create a cycle; an edge between two outside vertices does not extend the current tree.\n\nA crossing minimum is safe even if other, cheaper edges lie entirely outside. Prim maintains one connected growing tree, not several independently selected components. Ties among minimum crossing edges may be resolved either way unless a tie convention is specified.\n\n#### Table implementation and the Dijkstra distinction\n\nMaintain known/in-tree, tentative attachment cost d[w], and parent p[w]. Set the starting vertex's cost to 0, all others infinity. Select an unknown minimum d, mark it known, and update an unknown neighbor w if **weight(v,w)\u0026lt;d[w]**. Record v as its prospective parent.\n\nPrim compares the **single edge weight**, not d[v]+weight(v,w). Its d[w] is the cheapest connection from the growing tree to w; it is not total path distance from the start. Read final edges from each nonroot vertex and its parent.\n\n**Prim candidate for w = weight(v,w) | Dijkstra candidate = dist[v] + weight(v,w)**\n\n#### Correct setting and runtime\n\nThe standard problem is connected and undirected. If disconnected, one starting component cannot span all vertices using existing edges. Negative weights are allowed: Prim's safe crossing-edge argument does not use Dijkstra's nonnegative path-extension reasoning.\n\nArray-scan selection costs **O(V^2)**. With a binary heap on a connected sparse graph, the stated bound is **O(E log V)**. Store undirected edges at both endpoints in adjacency lists.\n\n**Prim: O(V^2) by array scan | O(E log V) with a binary heap on a connected graph**\n\nDifferent starts and tie choices can produce different edge sets when multiple MSTs exist, but all have the same minimum total cost. Adding a nontree edge to any spanning tree forms a cycle; removing an edge on that cycle restores a spanning tree, supporting exchange-style reasoning.\n\n\u003e **WHAT THE OLD QUIZ REQUIRES**\n\n\u003e Show which tree vertex set you currently have, which minimum crossing edge you select, and the new vertex it adds. A correct MST obtained by a different algorithm does not demonstrate the requested Prim execution.\n\n### Textbook seven-vertex MST, one crossing choice at a time\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 55 --\u003e\n*Weiss, Fig. 9.50, p. 394; stages Fig. 9.51, p. 395; tables Figs. 9.52-9.57, pp. 395-397.*\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"sg-figure\" data-source-page=\"55\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Textbook undirected weighted graph for Prim MST\" loading=\"lazy\"\u003e\u003c/figure\u003e\n\n*Textbook undirected graph, redrawn. Highlighted edges form its MST. Notice v4-v5 has weight 7 here; the similar Dijkstra graph uses a different directed weight.*\n\n| Step | Edge selected | Vertex added | Running cost |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Start | none | v1 | 0 |\n| 1 | v1-v4 (1) | v4 | 1 |\n| 2 | v1-v2 (2) | v2 | 3 |\n| 3 | v4-v3 (2) | v3 | 5 |\n| 4 | v4-v7 (4) | v7 | 9 |\n| 5 | v7-v6 (1) | v6 | 10 |\n| 6 | v7-v5 (6) | v5 | 16 |\n\nAfter v4 joins, v2 and v3 tie at attachment cost 2; the textbook selects v2 first. Later v7 lowers v6 from candidate 5 to edge cost 1, and v5 from 7 to 6. Final total is **1+2+2+4+1+6=16**.\n\nThe sequence of chosen edge weights need not increase: adding v7 makes the cheap v7-v6 edge newly eligible. Globally sorting all original edges is not the rule used by this trace.\n\n### Paper solution for one Prim crossing choice\n\u003c!-- section-role: worked-example; source-guide-page: 56 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n**Problem.** In the preceding textbook example, the current tree is {v1,v4}. Show the eligible edges and pick the next edge using the textbook tie choice.\n\n| Crossing edge | Weight |\n| --- | --- |\n| v1-v2 | 2 |\n| v1-v3 | 4 |\n| v4-v2 | 3 |\n| v4-v3 | 2 |\n| v4-v5 | 7 |\n| v4-v6 | 8 |\n| v4-v7 | 4 |\n\n**Solution.** The minimum crossing weight is 2. Choose v1-v2, adding v2. The tree now contains {v1,v4,v2} and its running cost is 1+2=3. Edge v1-v4 is internal, and v6-v7 is still outside at both ends.\n\n"}],"quickReference":"## Quick Reference\n\u003ca id=\"quick-reference\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- section-id: quick-reference --\u003e\n### Fast method-selection and runtime sheet\n\u003c!-- source-guide-page: 57 --\u003e\n*Summary of the verified assigned material. Bounds assume the stated standard representations and necessary preconditions.*\n\n| Problem / operation | Method or cost | Key qualification |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Binary-heap Find Min | O(1) | Root access |\n| Binary-heap Insert | Worst O(log N), average O(1) | Average assumptions; capacity available |\n| Binary-heap Delete Min | O(log N) | Replacement percolates down |\n| Bottom-up BuildHeap | O(N) | Sum height-dependent work |\n| d-heap Insert / Delete Min | O(log_d N) / O(d log_d N) | Scan children on downward steps |\n| Leftist merge / insert / Delete Min | O(log N) worst | Short right paths |\n| Binomial merge / Delete Min | O(log N) worst | O(log N) unique orders |\n| Binomial N inserts from empty | O(N) total worst | Not O(1) worst for every insertion |\n| Cuckoo lookup / deletion | O(1) worst | Two candidate cells |\n| Cuckoo insertion | Expected amortized O(1) | Hash/load assumptions and rebuilding |\n| Insertion sort | Best N; average/worst N^2 | Stable, O(1) auxiliary space |\n| Mergesort | O(N log N) | Stable merge, O(N) auxiliary array |\n| Heapsort | O(N log N) | O(1) iterative auxiliary space; unstable |\n| Quicksort | Average N log N; worst N^2 | Partition quality; stack space remains |\n| Comparison-sort lower bound | Omega(N log N) | General comparison-only model |\n| BFS / topological sort / DAG paths | O(V+E) | Adjacency lists |\n| Dijkstra / Prim array scan | O(V^2) | Different relaxation rules |\n| Negative-edge queue correction | O(VE) | No relevant negative cycle |\n| PERT timing | O(V+E) | DAG; forward max, backward min |\n| Arbitrary integral flow augmentation | O(fE) | f is numeric maximum flow value |\n\n### Heap index formulas for paper traces\n\u003c!-- source-guide-page: 58 --\u003e\n*Original paper-exam practice based on the assigned procedures.*\n\n| Convention | Parent of nonroot i | Children of i | Last internal position |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Binary heap, 1-based | floor(i/2) | 2i, 2i+1 | floor(N/2) |\n| d-heap, 1-based | floor((i-2)/d)+1 | d(i-1)+2 through d(i-1)+d+1 | parent(N) |\n| Binary heapsort, 0-based | floor((i-1)/2) | 2i+1, 2i+2 | floor(N/2)-1 |\n\nThese last-internal-position formulas assume N \u003e= 2. For heap repair, consider only children within the current active size. Mark the active boundary after every heapsort extraction.\n\n","sources":"## Sources and Attribution\n\u003ca id=\"sources\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n\u003c!-- section-id: sources --\u003e\n### Source map, scope, and example attribution\n\u003c!-- source-guide-page: 59 --\u003e\n*Page references throughout this guide use printed textbook pages, not PDF-viewer positions.*\n\n#### Primary assigned textbook\n\nMark Allen Weiss, **Data Structures and Algorithm Analysis in Java**, third edition, supplied full-text PDF. Each topic identifies its assigned pages and the verified figure numbers used for examples. Diagrams are redrawn; tables and narrative steps may expand the referenced example. Original numerical variations are explicitly labeled as original.\n\nBinary Heaps 225-232,234-237; Hashing 195,205; d-Heaps 240-241; Leftist 241-245; Binomial 252-259,261; Insertion Sort 272; Merge Sort 282-284; Heapsort 278-280,282; Quicksort 288-291,294; Lower Bounds 302-304; External Sorting 316-317; Graphs 359-362; Topological Sort 362-364, with the approved queue-pseudocode exception at 365; Dijkstra 372-377; Shortest Paths 366-370,372,380-381; Activity/critical-path material 380-384; Flow 386-392; Prim/MST portions 393-397.\n\n#### Supplied supplemental PDFs\n\n**Charles Chen, An Overview of Cuckoo Hashing (l13a.pdf), pp. 1-12.** Sections 3-4 explain collision policies and displacement insertion; Section 5 analyzes insertion/rebuilding; Section 6 describes universal families; Section 7 discusses experiments. The guide uses the stated probabilistic/load assumptions and avoids turning apparent proof/ratio typographical errors into rules.\n\n**Jeff Erickson, Algorithms Lecture 11: Efficient Heaps [Sp'24] (newfiboheap.pdf), pp. 1-11.** Sections 11.2-11.3 reinforce leftist/binomial heaps; Sections 11.4-11.6 cover lazy/Fibonacci and pairing heaps. The notes have unfinished references, an empty Lazy DecreaseKey heading, and apparent prose/comparison typos; this guide does not supply an invented missing algorithm or copy an order-violating link.\n\nErickson's notes state copyright 2024 Jeff Erickson and the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 license (creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/). The guide's adaptations of that supplemental material credit him and are offered on the same noncommercial share-alike terms. Textbook content remains attributed to Weiss.\n\n#### Syllabus and prior-quiz evidence\n\n**Fall 2026 Syllabus Version 2**, calendar on p. 4, supplies the reading boundaries. **Old Quiz 2**, April 13, 2026: Problem 1 asks algorithm suitability for traffic; Problem 2 asks linear BuildHeap and its explanation; Problem 3 asks heapsort direction; Problem 4 requires Prim; Problem 5 asks heap Java code explanation; Problem 6 asks PERT latest times and the approach. These are prioritization evidence, not predictions of the next quiz.\n\nSeparate Rosen pages, other lecture notes, and a specific PERT Wikipedia reading were not supplied. Their unseen contents are not claimed as summarized here. Boundary pages are used for the relevant assigned topic; adjacent Kruskal, replacement selection, and later algorithm code are not silently imported.\n\n"};

const StudyGuide=(()=>{
 const data=CS3345_STUDY_GUIDE_DATA,root=document.getElementById('studyGuideSection');
 const mapping={binarySection:'binary-heaps',dheapSection:'d-heaps',heapSection:'leftist-heaps',binomialSection:'binomial-trees-binomial-queues',insertionSection:'insertion-sort',mergeSection:'merge-sort',heapsortSection:'heapsort',quicksortSection:'quicksort',boundsSection:'sorting-lower-bounds',externalSection:'external-sorting',fundamentalsSection:'graph-fundamentals',topoSection:'topological-sort-dags',dijkstraSection:'dijkstras-algorithm',shortestSection:'shortest-path-algorithms',activitySection:'activity-graphs',pertSection:'pert-critical-path',flowSection:'network-flow',primSection:'prims-algorithm'};
 const byId=new Map(data.sections.map(x=>[x.id,x])),positions=new Map();let active=null;
 const node=(tag,text,cls)=>{const e=document.createElement(tag);if(text!==undefined)e.textContent=text;if(cls)e.className=cls;return e};
 const decode=s=>s.replace(/&(lt|gt|amp|quot|#39);/g,(_,e)=>({lt:'<',gt:'>',amp:'&',quot:'"','#39':"'"}[e]));
 function inline(parent,text){const pattern=/(`[^`]+`|\*\*[^*]+\*\*|\*[^*]+\*|\[[^\]]+\]\([^)]+\))/g;let last=0,m;while((m=pattern.exec(text))){if(m.index>last)parent.append(document.createTextNode(decode(text.slice(last,m.index))));const t=m[0];if(t[0]==='`')parent.append(node('code',decode(t.slice(1,-1))));else if(t.startsWith('**')){const b=node('strong');inline(b,t.slice(2,-2));parent.append(b)}else if(t[0]==='*'){const em=node('em');inline(em,t.slice(1,-1));parent.append(em)}else{const link=t.match(/^\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\)$/),a=node('a',decode(link[1]));if(/^(https?:\/\/|#)/.test(link[2])){a.href=link[2];if(link[2].startsWith('http'))a.target='_blank'}parent.append(a)}last=pattern.lastIndex}if(last<text.length)parent.append(document.createTextNode(decode(text.slice(last))))}
 const headingId=(id,line)=>'sg-'+id+'-'+line;
 function renderMarkdown(source,id){const host=node('div',undefined,'sg-reader'),lines=source.split(/\r?\n/);let i=0,worked=false;
  function block(parent,first,last){let n=first;while(n<last){const raw=lines[n],line=raw.trim();if(!line){n++;continue}if(/^<!--/.test(line)||/^<a id=/.test(line)){n++;continue}
   const heading=line.match(/^(#{1,4})\s+(.+)$/);if(heading){const h=node('h'+Math.max(2,Math.min(4,heading[1].length)));inline(h,heading[2]);h.id=headingId(id,n);h.dataset.sgLine=String(n);parent.append(h);n++;continue}
   const fig=line.match(/^<figure class="(sg-figure|sg-equation)" data-source-page="(\d+)"><img src="(data:image\/png;base64,[A-Za-z0-9+/=]+)" alt="([^"]*)" loading="lazy"><\/figure>$/);if(fig){const f=node('figure'),img=node('img');f.className=fig[1];f.dataset.sourcePage=fig[2];img.src=fig[3];img.alt=decode(fig[4]);img.loading='lazy';img.tabIndex=0;img.title='Open enlarged figure';img.onclick=()=>zoom(img);img.onkeydown=e=>{if(e.key==='Enter')zoom(img)};f.append(img);parent.append(f);n++;continue}
   if(/^\|/.test(line)){const rows=[];while(n<last&&/^\|/.test(lines[n].trim())){if(rows.length>1&&n+1<last&&/^\|[\s:|-]+\|$/.test(lines[n+1].trim()))break;rows.push(lines[n++].trim().slice(1,-1).split('|').map(x=>x.trim()))}const table=node('table'),wrap=node('div',undefined,'sg-table-scroll'),head=node('thead'),body=node('tbody');rows.forEach((cells,ri)=>{if(ri===1&&cells.every(x=>/^:?-+:?$/.test(x)))return;const tr=node('tr');for(const cell of cells){const c=node(ri===0?'th':'td');inline(c,cell);tr.append(c)}(ri===0?head:body).append(tr)});table.append(head,body);wrap.append(table);parent.append(wrap);continue}
   if(/^```/.test(line)){const pre=node('pre'),code=node('code');n++;const content=[];while(n<last&&!/^```/.test(lines[n].trim()))content.push(lines[n++]);code.textContent=content.join('\n');pre.append(code);parent.append(pre);n++;continue}
   if(/^>\s?/.test(line)){const text=line.trim().replace(/^>\s?/,'');if(/^\*\*[A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9 -]*\*\*$/.test(text)){parent.append(node('p',text.slice(2,-2),'sg-callout-label'));n++;continue}const quote=node('blockquote');while(n<last&&/^>\s?/.test(lines[n].trim())){const content=lines[n++].trim().replace(/^>\s?/,'');const p=node('p');inline(p,content);quote.append(p)}if(quote.childNodes.length)parent.append(quote);continue}
   if(/^(?:[-*]|\d+\.)\s+/.test(line)){const ordered=/^\d+\./.test(line),list=node(ordered?'ol':'ul');while(n<last&&/^(?:[-*]|\d+\.)\s+/.test(lines[n].trim())){const li=node('li');inline(li,lines[n++].trim().replace(/^(?:[-*]|\d+\.)\s+/,''));list.append(li)}parent.append(list);continue}
   const para=[];while(n<last&&lines[n].trim()&&!/^(?:#{1,4}\s|<!--|<a id=|<figure |\||```|>|[-*]\s|\d+\.\s)/.test(lines[n].trim()))para.push(lines[n++].trim());if(para.length){const p=node('p');inline(p,para.join(' '));parent.append(p)}else n++;
  }}
  const boundaries=[];for(let k=0;k<lines.length;k++)if(/^###\s/.test(lines[k]))boundaries.push(k);if(!boundaries.length){block(host,0,lines.length);window.q2FormatMathRoot?.(host);return host}block(host,0,boundaries[0]);for(let j=0;j<boundaries.length;j++){const start=boundaries[j],end=boundaries[j+1]??lines.length,section=node('section'),role=lines[start+1]?.match(/^<!-- section-role: ([a-z-]+)/)?.[1];if(role==='worked-example'){const solution=lines.findIndex((x,k)=>k>start&&k<end&&/^\*\*Solution\.\*\*/.test(x.trim()));if(solution>start){block(section,start,solution);const details=node('details',undefined,'sg-solution');details.append(node('summary','Show Worked Solution'));block(details,solution,end);section.append(details)}else block(section,start,end)}else block(section,start,end);host.append(section)}window.q2FormatMathRoot?.(host);return host}
 const get=key=>byId.get(key)||({id:key,title:key==='quick-reference'?'Quick Reference':'Sources and Attribution',markdown:key==='quick-reference'?data.quickReference:data.sources});
 const back=()=>{if(active)positions.set(active,scrollY);active=null;list();window.scrollTo(0,0)};
 function controls(key){const row=node('div',undefined,'sg-controls'),b=node('button','\u2190 Back to Study Guide');b.onclick=back;row.append(b);const at=data.sections.findIndex(x=>x.id===key);if(at>0){const prev=node('button','Previous Topic');prev.onclick=()=>open(data.sections[at-1].id);row.append(prev)}if(at>=0&&at<data.sections.length-1){const next=node('button','Next Topic');next.onclick=()=>open(data.sections[at+1].id);row.append(next)}return row}
 function open(key,target){if(active)positions.set(active,scrollY);active=key;const item=get(key);root.replaceChildren(controls(key),renderMarkdown(item.markdown,key),controls(key));showSection('studyGuide');requestAnimationFrame(()=>{const focus=target&&document.getElementById(headingId(key,target));if(focus)focus.scrollIntoView();else window.scrollTo(0,positions.get(key)||0)})}
 function search(query,results){results.replaceChildren();const term=query.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();if(!term)return;let found=0;for(const item of [...data.sections,{id:'quick-reference',title:'Quick Reference',markdown:data.quickReference}]){const lines=item.markdown.split(/\r?\n/);let heading=0;for(let i=0;i<lines.length;i++){const line=lines[i];if(/^#{2,4}\s/.test(line))heading=i;if(line.includes('data:image/png;base64,')||!line.toLocaleLowerCase().includes(term))continue;const btn=node('button',item.title),excerpt=node('small',line.replace(/^[#*>\s]+/,'').replace(/\*\*/g,'').slice(0,160));btn.append(excerpt);btn.onclick=()=>open(item.id,heading);results.append(btn);found++;if(found>=50)break}if(found>=50)break}if(!found)results.append(node('p','No results found.'))}
 function list(){root.replaceChildren();const crumb=node('div',undefined,'section-breadcrumb'),home=createHomeButton();crumb.append(home,node('span','/ Study Guide'));root.append(crumb,node('h1','Study Guide'),node('p','20 syllabus topics \u00b7 Concepts \u00b7 Worked examples \u00b7 Quick reference','sg-lead'));const about=node('details',undefined,'sg-intro-details');about.append(node('summary','About this guide'),renderMarkdown(data.intro,'intro'));root.append(about);const input=node('input');input.type='search';input.className='sg-search';input.placeholder='Search study guide...';input.setAttribute('aria-label','Search study guide');const results=node('div',undefined,'sg-search-results'),items=node('div',undefined,'sg-list');input.oninput=()=>{items.hidden=!!input.value.trim();search(input.value,results)};root.append(input,results,items);for(const item of data.sections){const b=node('button'),n=node('span',String(item.number).padStart(2,'0'),'sg-number');b.append(n,node('span',item.title),node('span','\u203a','sg-chevron'));b.onclick=()=>open(item.id);items.append(b)}for(const key of ['quick-reference','sources']){const b=node('button',get(key).title);b.onclick=()=>open(key);items.append(b)}}
 const zoomShell=node('div',undefined,'sg-zoom');zoomShell.hidden=true;zoomShell.setAttribute('role','dialog');zoomShell.setAttribute('aria-modal','true');zoomShell.setAttribute('aria-label','Enlarged study guide figure');let zoomFocus=null;function closeZoom(){zoomShell.hidden=true;zoomFocus?.focus();zoomFocus=null}document.body.append(zoomShell);
 function zoom(img){zoomFocus=document.activeElement;zoomShell.replaceChildren();const shade=node('button',undefined,'sg-backdrop'),large=node('img'),close=node('button','Close','sg-zoom-close');shade.type='button';shade.tabIndex=-1;shade.setAttribute('aria-label','Close enlarged figure');close.type='button';large.src=img.src;large.alt=img.alt;shade.onclick=closeZoom;large.onclick=closeZoom;close.onclick=closeZoom;zoomShell.append(shade,large,close);zoomShell.hidden=false;close.focus()}
 document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(!zoomShell.hidden){if(e.key==='Escape'){e.preventDefault();closeZoom()}else if(e.key==='Tab'){e.preventDefault();zoomShell.querySelector('.sg-zoom-close')?.focus()}}});
 for(const [section,id] of Object.entries(mapping)){const page=document.getElementById(section);if(!page)continue;const button=node('button','Study Guide','sg-practice-link');button.type='button';button.setAttribute('aria-label','Open '+byId.get(id).title+' study guide');button.onclick=()=>open(id);const breadcrumb=page.querySelector('.section-breadcrumb');if(breadcrumb)breadcrumb.append(button);else page.prepend(button)}
 list();return{open,list};
})();
